#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""simplmusik - a super simple music CLI.

Your library is the one music folder you choose, walked all the way down:
a subfolder isn't a level you browse, it's just where a file happens to sit.
Playlists are small JSON files in .simplmusik/playlists inside that folder -
so syncing it between machines takes your playlists with it - naming a song by
its filename and where it was last seen:

    {"name": "Road trip", "created": 1730000000, "played": 1730500000,
     "order": "newest",
     "songs": [{"file": "Killer Queen - Queen.mp3", "dir": "/home/me/Music"}]}

The filename is the identity everywhere; `dir` is only a shortcut. A song that
has moved is looked for again under your music folder once and the list rewritten
with where it turned up. One that is nowhere keeps its place, marked not found
- a drive you haven't plugged in yet is no reason to edit your playlist.

Everything reads newest first. Songs go by the file's own timestamp - when it
turned up in your folder - and a playlist's songs go by the order they are
written in, which is why adding one puts it at the top: the list is stored the
way it is read. `created` and `played` are what the sidebar sorts playlists by
(last played, or the day you made it); `order` says the list has been put
newest-first, and is what stops an older one being turned round twice.

Commands:
  status                     library + playback summary
  folder [set] [dir]         show your music folder, or choose another
                             (`folder set` on its own opens a folder picker)
  songs                      list every song in the library, newest first
  list [playlist]            list playlists, or the songs in one - playlists
                             by when you last played one, songs by when they
                             were added, both newest first
  create <name> [--cover P]  make a new, empty playlist
  rename <playlist> <name>   give a playlist a different name
  cover <playlist> [image]   show, set or (--clear) drop its cover art
  add <playlist> <file...>   add songs to a playlist, importing any that
                             aren't in the library yet
  search <query> [--count N] search YouTube for songs
  download <id>              download a search result into the library
  remove <playlist> <song>   drop a song from a playlist (the file stays)
  remove <song>              delete a song: the file, and every list naming it
  delete <playlist>          delete a playlist (its songs stay)
  play [playlist] [song]     play a playlist, one song, or the whole library
  levelling [on|off]         play every song at one loudness (on by default)
  target [dB]                where that loudness sits (-30 to -12, -20 default)
  volume [0-100]             how loud, on top of the levelling
  shuffle [on|off]           play in a random order, until you turn it off
  pause / resume             hold, and let go
  skip / back                next / previous song
  seek <time>                jump within the song (1:23, 83, +30, -10)
  stop                       stop playback
Add --json to any command for machine-readable output.

How playback works: there is one player. `play` starts a detached process that
owns the queue and drives one long-lived mpv, telling it a song at a time over
mpv's JSON IPC socket - and if one is already running, `play` hands it the new
queue instead of starting a second. What is playing is not written down
anywhere: it is that process, which holds an exclusive lock saying so and
answers a socket saying what. `status` here, and the window, and a second
terminal all ask it the same question and get the same answer, because there
is only one place the answer lives.

The player keeps two lists and a tally. `songs` is the pool it draws from;
`order` is what it has actually played, grown one song at a time, so it is the
history and what `back` walks. `plays` is how many times each song has been
played, kept per playlist - your library is a playlist here too, being a pool
like any other, and the same song sitting in two of them is two separate
things owed, because it is each list that has to come out even.

The draw is two rules, in this order. Nothing heard in the last half a
playlist, ever - which for two songs is the one you are on, so a pair still
alternates. Then, of what is left, the songs the playlist has played fewest,
and chance between those. So a playlist comes out even round by round, every
song once before any song twice, and the window decides how much freedom the
levelling has within that.

The two cannot deadlock. j songs into a round the fewest-played pool holds
N-j songs, while the window can bar at most N//2 - j of them, and N-j is
always the larger. Only a one-song playlist runs out of songs, and then it
plays the one song there is.

`heard` and `plays` are deliberately not the same list. Everything that comes
out of the speakers goes in `heard`, whoever chose it - your ears cannot tell
who picked the song, so neither does the window. Only choices the queue itself
made go in `plays`: a song the draw chose, and the song a playlist opens on,
since putting a list on is starting it and the song you start it on is a song
you have heard, whether you named it or the draw did. Picking a song out of a
playlist that is already going is the case that counts for nothing - that is
you moving about inside it, so the shuffle still owes that song what it owed
before.

Left to the draw, the counts are never more than one apart, because the only
thing that moves them takes a song from the bottom of the tally to the top.
Clicking about can put them two apart for a moment: a song you clicked is
barred by the window but was never counted, so the draw may have to play
something a second time while the song it owes waits out its half a playlist.
It comes back level as soon as that song is free again, and the window is the
rule that gives way to nothing.

A session is playing that never stopped, however you got there. It survives
skipping, pausing, and putting on another playlist - a queue is swapped under
the player, but the counts stay with the player, each list's own kept apart
from the rest. Come back to a playlist mid-session and it is owed what it was
owed. It ends when the music does: `stop`, or a queue running out, and the
next play starts everything at nought.

Shuffle itself is a setting, not a session. It sits in the config file beside
levelling, so it stays on until you turn it off and everything played from
then on - from here or from the window - is shuffled. `play --shuffle` is that
same switch thrown on the way past. Name a song and a shuffled play still
starts on it and draws from there on; name none and the opening song is a draw
like any other, counted like any other.

Seeking moves the mpv that is already playing, so a song carries on rather
than starting again. How far into a song we are is mpv's own `time-pos`, and
the socket is a file like any other, so anyone can ask it - `seek -10` here,
and the progress bar in the UI - with no clock kept anywhere to drift.

Every song is levelled to the same loudness. It is measured once - the mean
of its squared samples, and its loudest single sample - and mpv is handed a
`volume=NdB` filter to match, so nothing on disk is ever touched. The gain is
capped at whatever leaves that loudest sample just under full scale, so no song
can clip. `target` moves where they are all aimed; only the two measurements
are cached, never a gain, so aiming somewhere else re-measures nothing.
Measurements are kept in ~/.cache/simplmusik.json.

Control: every command - stop, pause, skip, back, shuffle, seek - is a line of
JSON to the player's socket, answered by the player. Nothing signals a pid and
nothing looks one up, because a pid written down is only a guess by the time
anything reads it, and means nothing outside the namespace that issued it.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 1 user error, 2 nothing found.
"""

import argparse, fcntl, json, os, random, re, select, shutil, signal, socket
import subprocess, sys, threading, time, zipfile

AUDIO = {".mp3", ".wav", ".flac", ".ogg", ".m4a", ".opus", ".aac"}
HOME_MUSIC = os.path.expanduser("~/Music")          # where playlists once sat
CONF = os.path.expanduser("~/.config/simplmusik")  # the simplmusik folder
CONFIG = os.path.join(CONF, "config.json")
HOMELESS = os.path.join(CONF, "playlists")          # where they waited, once
WAS = [HOMELESS, os.path.join(HOME_MUSIC, "playlists")]   # where they used to live
LOCK = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state/simplmusik-player.lock")
SOCK = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state/simplmusik-player.sock")
MPVSOCK = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state/simplmusik-mpv.sock")
JOB = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state/simplmusik-job.json")
# Where playback used to be written down, back when it was written down at all.
# Nothing reads these now; a player sweeps them up so an upgrade leaves nothing
# behind that looks like a player to a copy of this that hasn't been upgraded.
LEGACY = [os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state/simplmusik.json"),
          os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state/simplmusik-ctl.json")]
# Where the play log used to be, back when it was this machine's rather than
# the library's. `migrate` lifts it into the music folder, once.
WAS_PLAYS = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state/simplmusik-plays.jsonl")
DLDIR = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state/simplmusik-downloads")
RESOLVED = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state/simplmusik-resolved")
SHOWN = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state/simplmusik-shown.json")
CACHE = os.path.expanduser("~/.cache/simplmusik.json")
NULL = subprocess.DEVNULL

CONF_DOT = ".simplmusik"            # the hidden folder playlists sit in
PLAYS_NAME = "plays.jsonl"          # ...and the log of what you played, beside them

# The library, named. It is not a playlist and has no file - it is every song
# in your music folder, which is not a list anybody made - but it is the list
# the window shows at the top of its sidebar and the one `play` falls back to,
# so everything that takes a playlist name takes this one too. A playlist you
# actually called "Library" wins: `find` is always asked first, and a name you
# gave something of your own is never taken away from you.
LIBRARY_NAME = "All songs"
LIBRARY_NOTE = "default library"
LIBRARY_ALIASES = {"all songs", "all-songs", "allsongs", "all",
                   "library", "default library"}

def is_library(name):
    """Whether a name means the library rather than a playlist. Only ever
    asked after `find` has said there is no playlist by that name."""
    return (name or "").strip().lower() in LIBRARY_ALIASES

# realpath, not abspath: installed, this file is a symlink on the PATH into
# the folder holding the rest of us, and only the resolved path leads back to
# what ships alongside - the bundled yt-dlp included.
HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
YTDLP_BUNDLED = os.path.join(HERE, "yt-dlp")        # the one shipped with us
YTDLP_USER = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/share/simplmusik/yt-dlp")
YTDLP_URL = "https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp/releases/latest/download/yt-dlp"

# --------------------------------------------------------------- the player
# There is one player, and this is what makes it one.
#
# What is playing is not written down anywhere. It is held by the process
# doing the playing - the only thing that can't be wrong about it - and
# everything else asks that process over a socket it listens on. `status` in a
# terminal and the window both ask the same question of the same process and
# are told the same thing, because there is only one thing to be told. That is
# the whole of the design: one variable, in one place, with no second copy to
# fall out of step with it.
#
# Two rules keep it single, and both are the kernel's to enforce rather than
# ours to remember:
#
#   the lock    A player holds an exclusive flock on LOCK for as long as it
#               plays. Two processes cannot both hold it, so two players
#               cannot both exist - whatever raced, however fast you asked.
#               The kernel drops it when the process ends, however it ends, so
#               there is no stale lock to clear up after a crash.
#   the socket  "Is anything playing?" is "does anyone answer on SOCK?". A
#               socket whose process is gone refuses the connection outright,
#               so a dead player and a live one can never be confused.
#
# What this replaces is a pid in a file, and the reason it had to go is that a
# pid only means anything inside the namespace that issued it. Give two
# launches a namespace each and the player behind one window writes down that
# it is pid 283; the next window reads that, asks after its own pid 283 - a
# different process, or none - and concludes there is nothing playing. It can
# neither see the player nor signal it, so it starts a second one over the
# top, and a third, each one audible and none of them reachable. A lock and a
# socket are the filesystem's, and every copy of this app - package or a
# checkout run from a terminal - is looking at the same filesystem.

def load_json(path, default):
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            return json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return default

def save_json(path, obj, indent=None):
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
    with open(path + ".tmp", "w") as f:
        json.dump(obj, f, indent=indent)
    os.replace(path + ".tmp", path)    # atomic: no reader sees a half file

# The lock is held by keeping its file open, so the descriptor has to outlive
# the call that took it - hence a global. Closing it is the one way to let
# playback be started twice, so nothing here ever closes it.
_LOCK_FD = []

def hold_lock():
    """Take the one lock there is, or find out somebody else has it. True if
    this process is now the player, False if one already exists."""
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(LOCK), exist_ok=True)
        fd = os.open(LOCK, os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT, 0o600)
    except OSError:
        return False
    try:
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
    except OSError:                     # somebody is playing: not us, then
        os.close(fd)
        return False
    _LOCK_FD.append(fd)
    return True

def ask(req, timeout=2.0):
    """Put a question or an order to the player, and wait for its answer.

    None when there is no player to ask - nothing listening, or a socket file
    left behind by one that was killed, which refuses a connection rather than
    accepting it. Both are the same answer and neither is a guess, which is
    the point: this is the only test of whether anything is playing, and it
    works the same from a package or from a checkout, because a socket is a
    file and we are all looking at the same disk."""
    try:
        s = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX)
        s.settimeout(timeout)
        s.connect(SOCK)
    except OSError:
        return None
    try:
        s.sendall(json.dumps(req).encode() + b"\n")
        buf = b""
        while b"\n" not in buf:
            got = s.recv(65536)
            if not got:                 # hung up mid-answer: no answer
                return None
            buf += got
        return json.loads(buf.split(b"\n", 1)[0])
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    finally:
        s.close()

def playing(timeout=2.0):
    """What is playing, as the one process that knows has it. {} if nothing
    is. Everything in here that used to read the state file reads this."""
    r = ask({"op": "now"}, timeout)
    return r if r and r.get("playing") else {}

def send(op, **kw):
    """Tell the player to do something. False if there is nothing playing,
    which is not an error anywhere it is used - there is simply nothing to
    tell."""
    r = ask(dict(op=op, **kw))
    return bool(r and r.get("ok"))

def quit_stale_mpv():
    """Ask whatever is on the mpv socket to quit, and take the socket away.

    An mpv outlives its player only when the player was killed outright, and
    then it is still holding the sound card while nothing at all is driving
    it. Whoever is listening on that socket is that mpv, by definition - so it
    is asked by name rather than by pid, which is what makes this work across
    a sandbox boundary that a signal could never cross."""
    mpv_line(["quit"])
    for path in [MPVSOCK] + LEGACY:
        try:
            os.remove(path)
        except OSError:
            pass

def stop_player():
    """Stop playback. True if something was actually playing."""
    if not ask({"op": "stop"}):
        quit_stale_mpv()        # nobody to stop, but perhaps something to tidy
        return False
    for _ in range(40):         # up to 2s for it to tear its mpv down and go
        if not ask({"op": "now"}, 0.2):
            return True
        time.sleep(0.05)
    return True

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- cache
# Anything about a song that costs something to work out, kept so it is worked
# out once. Keyed by filename - a song's identity everywhere here - and stamped
# with the file's size and mtime, so a song that moves folders keeps what we
# know about it and one that is re-encoded or replaced quietly loses it. It is
# only ever a cache: delete the file and the only cost is the work being done
# again.

def stamp(path):
    """What makes a file the same file: its size and when it changed."""
    try:
        st = os.stat(path)
        return [st.st_size, int(st.st_mtime)]
    except OSError:
        return None

def cached(name, path):
    """What we know about this song, or {} if the file isn't what it was."""
    e = load_json(CACHE, {}).get(name) or {}
    return e if e.get("stamp") == stamp(path) else {}

def remember(name, path, **facts):
    """Add to what we know about a song. The whole file is rewritten, which
    costs nothing at library size and keeps the write atomic."""
    s = stamp(path)
    if s is None:               # the file went away: nothing worth keeping
        return
    c = load_json(CACHE, {})
    e = c.get(name) or {}
    if e.get("stamp") != s:     # a different file under the same name
        e = {"stamp": s}
    e.update(facts)
    c[name] = e
    save_json(CACHE, c)

# --------------------------------------------------------------- the picker
# Picking a folder is the desktop's job, not ours: `folder set` with nothing
# after it asks the desktop for one, which is what the button in the window
# does. What comes back joins the same path a typed one takes, so there is one
# way the folder is chosen and not two. zenity is what Mint has; kdialog is what a
# KDE machine has instead. No display means no dialog, so don't offer one.

CHOOSERS = [["zenity", "--file-selection", "--directory",
             "--title", "Choose a music folder"],
            ["kdialog", "--getexistingdirectory", os.path.expanduser("~")]]

def chooser():
    """The command that opens this desktop's folder picker, or None."""
    if not (os.environ.get("DISPLAY") or os.environ.get("WAYLAND_DISPLAY")):
        return None
    for cmd in CHOOSERS:
        if shutil.which(cmd[0]):
            return cmd
    return None

def choose_folder():
    """Ask the desktop for a folder: the path, "" if the dialog was closed
    without one, or None if there is no picker here to open. No timeout - a
    dialog is open for exactly as long as someone is looking at it."""
    cmd = chooser()
    if not cmd:
        return None
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(cmd, capture_output=True, text=True)
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    return p.stdout.strip() if p.returncode == 0 else ""

# ------------------------------------------------------- library + playlists
# The library is the one music folder you have chosen, walked all the way
# down. Subfolders are disregarded as structure: what comes back is one flat
# list of songs, however they happen to be arranged on disk. A song's filename
# is its identity everywhere - in a playlist, in the state file, in the UI -
# and the first subfolder holding a name wins, so the same name twice is one
# song rather than an ambiguity to settle again at every turn.

def xdg_music():
    """Where this desktop keeps music, or None if it doesn't say.

    ~/Music is only the answer on an English machine - a German one says
    ~/Musik, and somebody who moved theirs onto another disk says that here
    too. The file is one shell assignment a line, so it is read rather than
    shelled out to `xdg-user-dir`: this is asked before every command.

    $HOME on its own is how the file says "no music folder", and taking it at
    its word would make the library the whole of your home directory - so that
    one answer is declined."""
    home = os.path.expanduser("~")
    conf = os.environ.get("XDG_CONFIG_HOME") or os.path.join(home, ".config")
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(conf, "user-dirs.dirs")) as f:
            for line in f:
                m = re.match(r'\s*XDG_MUSIC_DIR\s*=\s*"(.*)"\s*$', line)
                if m:
                    d = os.path.abspath(m.group(1).replace("$HOME", home))
                    return d if d != home else None
    except OSError:
        pass                        # no file, no desktop, nothing to go on
    return None

def default_folder():
    """The music folder for somebody who has never chosen one.

    The desktop's own answer comes first, because a folder that already holds
    your music is a better default than a name we made up - and the best
    folder picker is the one nobody ever has to open. It gives way to ~/Music
    in one case: simplmusik already living there, which is somebody upgrading,
    and moving a library out from under them is not a default's job. Where
    neither folder is on the disk yet, ~/Music is the name to make."""
    home = os.path.expanduser("~/Music")
    xdg = xdg_music()
    if not xdg or xdg == home or os.path.isdir(os.path.join(home, CONF_DOT)):
        return home
    return xdg if os.path.isdir(xdg) else home

def folder():
    """Your music folder: the one place a song can be, and where new ones
    land. There is always one - never having chosen means whatever this
    desktop calls its music folder - so nothing downstream has to have an
    answer for having none."""
    c = load_json(CONFIG, {})
    d = c.get("folder")
    if not d and isinstance(c.get("folders"), list) and c["folders"]:
        d = c["folders"][0]         # a config from when this was a list
    return os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(str(d or default_folder())))

def setting(key, default):
    """One setting from the config file, or what it is when nobody has said."""
    v = load_json(CONFIG, {}).get(key)
    return default if v is None else v

def set_setting(key, value):
    """Change one setting and leave the rest of the file alone - it is yours to
    edit by hand, so it is written back indented and nothing else moves."""
    c = load_json(CONFIG, {})
    c[key] = value
    save_json(CONFIG, c, indent=2)

def set_folder(path):
    set_setting("folder", path)

def under(path, root):
    """Is this path inside the music folder? What a playlist remembers about
    a song is only a shortcut, and one pointing outside the library is no
    shortcut at all - a song there is not one of yours, whatever is still on
    the disk at that address, so it has to be looked up like any other."""
    path = os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(path))
    return path == root or path.startswith(root + os.sep)

def arrived(path):
    """When a song turned up in your library.

    The file's own timestamp, and nothing written down beside it: a download
    stamps itself as it is written, an import is copied rather than cloned so
    it dates from the import, and a folder synced to another machine carries
    its timestamps across - so both machines read the library in the same
    order, without a record either of them has to keep. A file we can't ask
    about is 0, which is the bottom of the list: where something we know
    nothing about belongs."""
    try:
        return os.path.getmtime(path)
    except OSError:
        return 0.0

def index():
    """Every song you have, as {filename: full path}, newest first.

    The walk itself stays alphabetical, so which of two files with one name
    wins is decided the way it always was; the order is put on afterwards.
    Newest first is the order everything shows - `songs`, the window's All
    songs, and playing the library - because it is decided here, once."""
    out = {}
    for here, dirs, names in os.walk(folder()):
        dirs[:] = sorted(d for d in dirs if not d.startswith("."))
        for n in sorted(names):
            if os.path.splitext(n)[1].lower() in AUDIO and n not in out:
                out[n] = os.path.join(here, n)
    return dict(sorted(out.items(),
                       key=lambda kv: (-arrived(kv[1]), kv[0].lower())))

def library():
    """Every song in the library: one flat list, newest first."""
    return list(index())

def playlists_dir():
    """Where playlists are kept: inside your music folder, so syncing that
    folder between machines carries them along with the music they name.
    The folder is hidden, which is exactly what keeps it out of the library -
    `index` walks past dot-directories, so playlists are never mistaken for
    something to play."""
    return os.path.join(folder(), CONF_DOT, "playlists")

def cache_dir():
    """Where a song lands while it is still coming down.

    It sits inside the hidden folder for two reasons. `index` walks past
    dot-directories, so a half-written song is never a library song - there is
    no window in which the library contains something that can't be played.
    And it is inside your music folder rather than off in /tmp, which puts it
    on the same filesystem as the library: the move that finishes a download
    is then a rename, which either has happened or hasn't, instead of a copy
    that can be interrupted halfway."""
    return os.path.join(folder(), CONF_DOT, "cache")

def plays_path():
    """Every song you have played, kept with the library rather than with this
    machine. It is a record *of* your library - what you played and what you
    skipped - so a folder synced between two machines should carry it along
    the way it carries the playlists, rather than leaving each machine with
    half a history and neither with the whole of one.

    Hidden, beside the playlists, so `index` walks past it: a log is never a
    song. It is not in the cache folder next door because nothing regenerates
    it - lose it and those evenings are simply gone."""
    return os.path.join(folder(), CONF_DOT, PLAYS_NAME)

def remote(path):
    """Whether this is something mpv fetches rather than a file on disk.

    A stream is handed to mpv exactly the way a path is, so most of the player
    never learns the difference. The places that do are the ones that want to
    open the file themselves - and there is no file yet."""
    return bool(path) and path.startswith(("http://", "https://"))

def sweep_cache(days=1):
    """Drop cache files nothing is coming back for. A fetch killed partway
    leaves its part-file behind, and nothing else would ever remove it -
    the finished ones move out of here the moment they are whole."""
    cutoff = time.time() - days * 86400
    try:
        for n in os.listdir(cache_dir()):
            f = os.path.join(cache_dir(), n)
            try:
                if os.path.isfile(f) and os.path.getmtime(f) < cutoff:
                    os.remove(f)
            except OSError:
                pass
    except OSError:
        pass

def lift(old, now):
    """Move something that has changed address. Nothing happens if it was
    never at the old one, or if something is already at the new - so this can
    be asked on every command and only ever acts the once. True if it moved.

    Nothing here is worth failing a command over: a playlist that could not be
    carried across stays readable where it is, which is the whole of the
    damage, and asking again tomorrow tries again."""
    if (not os.path.exists(old) or os.path.exists(now)
            or os.path.abspath(old) == os.path.abspath(now)):
        return False
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(now), exist_ok=True)
        shutil.move(old, now)
        return True
    except OSError:
        return False

def drain_plays():
    """Bring the play log in from the address it used to be kept at.

    Appended rather than moved, which is the one way this differs from every
    other move here: both files can hold lines worth keeping. A player started
    before this version is still running and still writing to the old address,
    and the evening it is in the middle of should not be stranded there
    because some other command migrated the file out from under it. So the old
    file is emptied into the new one and removed, and a player that writes to
    it again simply has it drained again next time.

    Appending is the one thing this format is built for, so there is nothing
    to merge: the lines each carry the moment they describe, and anything
    reading them that cares about order sorts on `at` rather than trusting the
    file. A last line cut short by a crash is closed before the join, so a
    half-written record can never swallow the first good one after it."""
    now = plays_path()
    if (not os.path.isfile(WAS_PLAYS)
            or os.path.abspath(WAS_PLAYS) == os.path.abspath(now)):
        return
    try:
        with open(WAS_PLAYS, "rb") as f:
            was = f.read()
        if was:
            if not was.endswith(b"\n"):
                was += b"\n"
            os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(now), exist_ok=True)
            fd = os.open(now, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_APPEND, 0o600)
            with os.fdopen(fd, "ab") as f:
                f.write(was)        # only removed if this got there
        os.remove(WAS_PLAYS)
    except OSError:
        pass                        # it stays where it is, and is tried again

def rehome(was, log):
    """Follow the music folder. The playlists and the record of what you have
    played both live inside it, so choosing a different one has to take them
    along - otherwise they are left behind in a folder nothing looks at any
    more, out of sight.

    The cache next door is not carried. It holds fetches in flight and songs
    on their way into the folder they were asked for, which is the one being
    left; there is nothing in it that the new folder is missing."""
    lift(was, playlists_dir())
    lift(log, plays_path())

def stems():
    """Every playlist file, by name. One place decides what counts as one."""
    d = playlists_dir()
    return [f for f in (sorted(os.listdir(d)) if os.path.isdir(d) else [])
            if f.endswith(".json")]

def playlist_file(stem):
    return os.path.join(playlists_dir(), stem + ".json")

def meta(stem):
    return load_json(playlist_file(stem), {})

def write_meta(stem, m):
    os.makedirs(playlists_dir(), exist_ok=True)
    with open(playlist_file(stem), "w") as f:
        json.dump(m, f, indent=2)

def entry(e):
    """One stored song, as (filename, the folder it was last seen in)."""
    if isinstance(e, dict):
        return os.path.basename(e.get("file") or ""), (e.get("dir") or "")
    return os.path.basename(e or ""), ""    # older lists stored a bare name

def store(songs):
    """Songs on their way back into a playlist file: the name, and where it
    was found, so looking it up next time is one stat and not a search."""
    return [{"file": s["file"], "dir": os.path.dirname(s["path"])} if s["path"]
            else {"file": s["file"]} for s in songs]

def newest_first(stem, m):
    """Put a playlist's songs newest first, once, and say whether that changed
    anything.

    The file's order is the order: the CLI, the window and a playlist opened in
    an editor all read one list, and playing it plays it as it reads - so there
    is nothing to keep in step, and no view that disagrees with what is stored.
    Adding a song puts it on the top (see `cmd_add`), which is the whole of the
    bookkeeping; where a song sits is when it was added.

    Lists made before this was so were written oldest first, which is the same
    list backwards. Turning one round is a one-time job, and `order` is how it
    is known to be done - without it a list would be turned again on every
    read. The date the file carried up to now is kept as the date it was made,
    because this rewrite is about to replace it and it is the only record of
    when the playlist appeared."""
    if m.get("order") == "newest":
        return False
    m["order"] = "newest"
    m["songs"] = list(reversed(m.get("songs") or []))
    if not m.get("created"):
        try:
            m["created"] = round(os.path.getmtime(playlist_file(stem)), 3)
        except OSError:
            pass                # nothing to keep: it falls back to today
    return True

def songs_of(stem):
    """A playlist's songs, each as {"file": name, "path": where it is now}.

    The remembered folder is tried first, so the usual case costs one stat -
    but only if it is inside your music folder, so that choosing a different
    one doesn't leave a playlist quietly playing out of the old one. When it
    misses - the song moved, or the folder did - your music folder is searched
    and the list is rewritten with where it turned up, so that search happens
    once rather than once per playback. A song nowhere under it stays
    in the list with path None: the list is what you made, and a file that is
    off the disk today may be back tomorrow, so nothing is quietly dropped.
    Duplicates are, though - shuffle's 'not played lately' check reads the
    list and one name twice in it would fool that.

    The list comes back in the order it is written in, which is newest first:
    see `newest_first`."""
    m = meta(stem)
    root, idx, out, seen = folder(), None, [], set()
    moved = newest_first(stem, m)
    for e in m.get("songs") or []:
        name, was = entry(e)
        if not name or name in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(name)
        path = os.path.join(was, name) if was and under(was, root) else ""
        if not (path and os.path.isfile(path)):
            idx = index() if idx is None else idx
            path, moved = idx.get(name), True
        out.append({"file": name, "path": path or None})
    if moved:
        m["songs"] = store(out)
        write_meta(stem, m)
    return out

# ------------------------------------------------------------- cover art
# A playlist's own picture, kept beside its JSON as <stem>.<kind> - so the
# folder you sync carries the artwork along with the list, and a playlist
# deleted takes its picture with it rather than leaving one behind.

COVER_MAX = 8 << 20             # 8 MB: a cover, not a photo library

def image_kind(path):
    """What kind of image a file holds, read from the first bytes rather than
    the name on it. An extension is only a claim, and this picture is handed
    straight back to a browser, so the file itself is what decides."""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            head = f.read(16)
    except OSError:
        return None
    if head[:3] == b"\xff\xd8\xff":                     return "jpg"
    if head[:8] == b"\x89PNG\r\n\x1a\n":                return "png"
    if head[:4] == b"GIF8":                             return "gif"
    if head[:4] == b"RIFF" and head[8:12] == b"WEBP":   return "webp"
    return None

def cover_path(stem):
    """Where a playlist's cover art is, or None if it hasn't any. The name in
    the file is taken as a basename, so a playlist can only ever point at a
    picture in the playlists folder and never anywhere else on the disk."""
    name = os.path.basename((meta(stem).get("cover") or "").strip())
    path = os.path.join(playlists_dir(), name) if name else ""
    return path if path and os.path.isfile(path) else None

def drop_cover(stem):
    """Take a playlist's picture off it, and delete the picture. True if there
    was one. The file is ours - we copied it in - so removing it is not the
    same as removing something of yours."""
    path, m = cover_path(stem), meta(stem)
    had = bool(m.get("cover"))
    if path:
        try:
            os.remove(path)
        except OSError:
            pass                # gone already, or read-only: the list still lets go
    if had:
        m.pop("cover", None)
        write_meta(stem, m)
    return had

def set_cover(stem, src):
    """Give a playlist a picture, copied in beside its list. Returns where it
    landed, or raises ValueError saying why it couldn't. Copying rather than
    pointing at where you got it is what makes the artwork survive tidying up
    your Downloads folder - and travel with the list when the folder syncs."""
    if not os.path.isfile(src):
        raise ValueError("no such image: %s" % src)
    kind = image_kind(src)
    if not kind:
        # No name in this: the window hands the picture over as a temporary
        # file, and its name would mean nothing at all to the person reading.
        raise ValueError("that isn't a JPEG, PNG, GIF or WebP image")
    if os.path.getsize(src) > COVER_MAX:
        raise ValueError("that image is bigger than %d MB" % (COVER_MAX >> 20))
    dest = os.path.join(playlists_dir(), stem + "." + kind)
    if os.path.abspath(src) != os.path.abspath(dest):
        drop_cover(stem)        # the old picture goes, whatever it was called
        os.makedirs(playlists_dir(), exist_ok=True)
        # The contents and nothing else. Copying the mode across would leave a
        # cover picked in the window readable only by you - it arrives here as
        # a temporary file, and those are private - while the list beside it is
        # an ordinary file. A playlist and its picture travel together, so they
        # are written the same way.
        shutil.copyfile(src, dest)
    m = meta(stem)
    m["cover"] = os.path.basename(dest)
    write_meta(stem, m)
    return dest

def recency(stem, m):
    """Where a playlist stands in the order: when you last put it on, or - if
    you never have - when you made it.

    Two dates rather than one because a playlist you have never played still
    has to sit somewhere, and the day you made it is what you know about it;
    so a new playlist opens at the top and drifts down as others are played,
    rather than starting at the bottom of a list you made it to be at the top
    of. One kept somewhere this app has never written to has neither date, and
    the file's own timestamp stands in for both."""
    for k in ("played", "created"):
        v = m.get(k)
        if isinstance(v, (int, float)) and v > 0:
            return float(v)
    try:
        return os.path.getmtime(playlist_file(stem))
    except OSError:
        return 0.0

def mark_played(stem):
    """Remember that this is the playlist you just put on - which is what
    lifts it to the top of the sidebar. Written when playback starts and not
    again as it runs: it is 'the last one you chose', not 'the one that has
    been sounding longest'."""
    m = meta(stem)
    m["played"] = round(time.time(), 3)
    write_meta(stem, m)

def playlists():
    """Every playlist, most recently played first - the order the sidebar, the
    add-to menu and `list` all read in, decided here so there is one answer to
    it and not one per screen.

    `songs_of` comes first because it settles the file, dates included, and
    `recency` has to read what it settled rather than what was there before."""
    out = []
    for f in stems():
        stem = f[:-5]
        songs = songs_of(stem)
        m = meta(stem)
        out.append({"file": stem, "name": m.get("name") or stem,
                    "cover": cover_path(stem), "songs": songs,
                    "recent": recency(stem, m)})
    out.sort(key=lambda p: (-p["recent"], p["name"].lower()))
    return out

def find(name):
    """A playlist's file stem, by stem or by display name. None if absent.
    Only the names are read: resolving every song to answer this would walk
    your whole music folder, and `find` is on the way to nearly every command."""
    name = os.path.basename((name or "").rstrip("/"))
    if name.endswith(".json"):
        name = name[:-5]
    if name and os.path.isfile(playlist_file(name)):
        return name
    for f in stems():
        if (meta(f[:-5]).get("name") or f[:-5]) == name:
            return f[:-5]
    return None

def filed_under(song):
    """The playlist a song was most recently filed in, or None if none names
    it. `find` asks where a playlist is by its name; this asks the same of a
    song, which is the question a stream has when it ends.

    Written rather than played: filing a song is writing the file, so the
    file's own timestamp is the record of when you did it, and the newest of
    them is where you last put this song. Only the stored names are read -
    `songs_of` resolves every one of them and writes back what it found, which
    would stamp every playlist it looked at with the moment it was asked and
    leave nothing to tell them apart by."""
    best = None
    for f in stems():
        stem = f[:-5]
        if not any(entry(e)[0] == song for e in meta(stem).get("songs") or []):
            continue
        try:
            when = os.path.getmtime(playlist_file(stem))
        except OSError:
            continue                    # gone since `stems` listed it
        if best is None or when > best[0]:
            best = (when, stem)
    return best[1] if best else None

def migrate():
    """Three things have changed shape. The config kept a list of music folders
    and now keeps one, so a list is collapsed to its first - which is already
    where playlists lived and where new songs landed, so nothing moves.

    Playlists themselves have moved twice: out of ~/Music/playlists, which
    tied them to the one music folder; and then into the music folder itself,
    so that a folder synced between machines brings its playlists along.

    And the play log has made that same journey since, for that same reason.
    It began beside the lock and the socket in ~/.local/state, which is this
    machine's and no part of any library - so syncing your music to a second
    machine left the history behind on the first, and neither of them had the
    whole of it. It is a record of this library and it travels with it.

    Each happens once, the first time anything runs after it."""
    c = load_json(CONFIG, {})
    if "folders" in c:
        dirs = c.pop("folders")
        c.setdefault("folder", dirs[0] if isinstance(dirs, list) and dirs
                     else default_folder())
        save_json(CONFIG, c, indent=2)
    # Both moves below are *into* the music folder, so both are asked after
    # the collapse above and not before: which folder that is has only just
    # been settled.
    drain_plays()
    now = playlists_dir()
    for old in WAS:
        if lift(old, now):
            break                   # the first one they were at is the one

def emit(as_json, obj, text):
    if as_json:
        print(json.dumps(obj, indent=2))
    elif text:
        print(text)

def parse_time(s):
    """A position in a song: seconds (83), clock time (1:23, 1:02:03), or a
    jump from where we are (+30, -10). None if it isn't a time at all."""
    s = (s or "").strip()
    rel = s[:1] in ("+", "-")
    sign, body = (-1 if s[:1] == "-" else 1), (s[1:] if rel else s)
    if not body:
        return None
    total = 0.0
    for part in body.split(":"):
        try:
            v = float(part)
        except ValueError:
            return None
        if v < 0:
            return None
        total = total * 60 + v      # so 1:02:03 falls out of the same loop
    return sign * total, rel

def clock(secs):
    return "%d:%02d" % (int(secs) // 60, int(secs) % 60)

def fail(as_json, msg, code):
    emit(as_json, {"error": msg}, msg)
    return code

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ loudness
# Songs are mastered at wildly different levels - a 16 dB spread across an
# ordinary library, which is a volume knob you keep having to touch. So every
# song is measured once and played through mpv's volume filter. Nothing is
# written to the file: the gain is worked out from the numbers each time, so
# only the measuring is cached and aiming somewhere else later re-measures
# nothing.
#
# The measure is the mean squared sample in dB, which ffmpeg calls mean_volume,
# and the target is where every song is aimed - -20 dBFS unless you move it.
# The catch is that a song quiet on
# average isn't always a quiet master - some already touch full scale on a peak
# - and boosting one of those would clip it. So the gain is capped at whatever
# leaves the loudest sample just under full scale, which is what makes clipping
# impossible here rather than unlikely: a song that can't reach the target
# stays a little short instead, and at the default target almost none have to.
# That is the whole of what moving the target trades: aim louder and the songs
# with the least headroom start falling short of the rest.

def shuffling():
    """Shuffle as a setting: on until you turn it off, so it outlives any one
    playback session and decides what the next `play` does."""
    return bool(setting("shuffle", False))

def levelling():
    """Whether songs are levelled to one loudness. On unless turned off."""
    return bool(setting("levelling", True))

def volume():
    """How loud, 0-100, and a setting rather than a session - the same kind of
    switch as levelling and shuffle, so it stays where you left it.

    It multiplies whatever levelling decided rather than arguing with it: one
    settles the difference between songs, the other how loud the lot of it is.
    100 is the song exactly as levelling left it, and there is nothing above
    100 on purpose - the gain is already capped so that no song can clip, and
    a boost here is the one thing that could undo that.

    The scale is mpv's own, which is cubic: 50 is a quarter of the way up in
    amplitude, not half. That is what makes a slider feel even to a listener
    rather than crowding every useful setting into the bottom of its travel."""
    try:
        return max(0, min(100, int(setting("volume", 100))))
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return 100          # a config hand-edited into nonsense still plays

TARGET = -20.0      # dBFS mean square every song is aimed at, until you move it
TARGET_LO = -30.0   # as quiet as it goes: every song reaches this one
TARGET_HI = -12.0   # as loud: a fair few songs have to fall short of this one
CEILING = -0.1      # dBFS the loudest sample may reach once the gain is on

def target():
    """Where levelling aims, in dBFS mean square - a setting like the volume
    and the levelling switch itself, so it stays where you left it, and held
    inside the two ends on the way out, so a config hand-edited past either
    still plays.

    Moving it costs no measuring. What is cached about a song is its mean and
    its peak, never a gain, so aiming somewhere else is arithmetic on numbers
    we already have."""
    try:
        return min(TARGET_HI, max(TARGET_LO,
                                  round(float(setting("target", TARGET)), 1)))
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return TARGET

def measure(path):
    """A song's mean square and its peak, both in dBFS, from one decode pass -
    around two seconds for a three minute song, which is why it happens once.
    None if the file can't be measured, which plays it as it is."""
    try:
        out = subprocess.run(
            ["ffmpeg", "-nostdin", "-hide_banner", "-nostats", "-i", path,
             "-map", "0:a:0", "-af", "volumedetect", "-f", "null", "-"],
            stdin=NULL, stdout=NULL, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
            timeout=300).stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace")
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    mean = re.search(r"mean_volume: *(-?[\d.]+) dB", out)
    peak = re.search(r"max_volume: *(-?[\d.]+) dB", out)
    return (float(mean.group(1)), float(peak.group(1))) if mean and peak else None

def gain(name, path):
    """How many dB to move this song by: what the target asks for, or what the
    peak allows, whichever is less. 0.0 when there is nothing to go on, so a
    song we can't measure - or levelling turned off - plays as it did before."""
    if not levelling() or remote(path):
        # A stream has no file to measure, and measuring one would mean
        # fetching the whole song before a note of it played - which is the
        # one thing streaming is for. It plays as it is, and the copy landing
        # in the background is measured and levelled the next time it is
        # played, by which point it is an ordinary library song.
        return 0.0              # off: hand mpv the song exactly as it is
    e = cached(name, path)
    if "rms" not in e:
        got = measure(path)
        if not got:
            return 0.0
        e = {"rms": got[0], "peak": got[1]}
        remember(name, path, **e)
    return round(min(target() - e["rms"], CEILING - e["peak"]), 1)

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------- mpv
# One mpv plays everything, for the whole session, driven over the JSON IPC
# socket it opens on request: a line of JSON in, a line of JSON back, and
# events pushed as they happen. That is the whole of the change from running a
# player per song - but it is what lets a song be moved, paused and relevelled
# where it stands, because the process playing it is still there to be asked.
#
# It is started with --no-config on purpose. An mpv.conf belongs to the person
# who wrote it, and a `volume=50` or an `af=` in one would quietly become part
# of how this app sounds; the levelling here is the only thing that should.

MPV_ARGS = ["--idle=yes",          # outlive every song, waiting for the next
            "--no-video",          # audio only, cover art included
            "--no-terminal",       # we are not a terminal it can read keys from
            "--no-config",         # nobody's mpv.conf, only ours
            "--keep-open=no"]      # a finished song ends, rather than pausing

def mpv_line(cmd, timeout=0.3):
    """One command to the running mpv, and its answer. Opens its own
    connection and drops it: the socket is a file, so `status` in one terminal
    can ask what is playing without going anywhere near the player process.
    None if there is no mpv, or it doesn't answer promptly - either way, the
    caller's answer is that it doesn't know."""
    try:
        s = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX)
        s.settimeout(timeout)
        s.connect(MPVSOCK)
    except OSError:
        return None
    try:
        s.sendall(json.dumps({"command": cmd, "request_id": 1}).encode() + b"\n")
        buf = b""
        while True:
            got = s.recv(65536)
            if not got:
                return None
            buf += got
            while b"\n" in buf:
                line, buf = buf.split(b"\n", 1)
                if not line.strip():
                    continue
                m = json.loads(line)
                # mpv pushes its events down this socket too, and one can land
                # between the question and the answer. Ours is the one wearing
                # the request id; anything else is passed over.
                if m.get("request_id") == 1:
                    return m
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    finally:
        s.close()
    return None

def ask_mpv(prop):
    """One property of whatever is playing, or None."""
    m = mpv_line(["get_property", prop])
    return m.get("data") if m and m.get("error") == "success" else None

def mpv_props(*names, timeout=0.3):
    """Several properties over one connection, so the position and the length
    of a song are read from the same instant rather than a round trip apart.
    Missing ones are simply absent: mpv with nothing loaded answers `duration`
    with an error, which is not a failure worth reporting anywhere."""
    out = {}
    try:
        s = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX)
        s.settimeout(timeout)
        s.connect(MPVSOCK)
    except OSError:
        return out
    try:
        for i, name in enumerate(names):
            s.sendall(json.dumps({"command": ["get_property", name],
                                  "request_id": i + 1}).encode() + b"\n")
        buf, seen = b"", 0
        while seen < len(names):
            got = s.recv(65536)
            if not got:
                break
            buf += got
            while b"\n" in buf:
                line, buf = buf.split(b"\n", 1)
                if not line.strip():
                    continue
                m = json.loads(line)
                rid = m.get("request_id")
                # mpv pushes its events down this socket too, and one can land
                # between the questions and the answers. Ours wear request ids.
                if not isinstance(rid, int) or not 1 <= rid <= len(names):
                    continue
                seen += 1
                if m.get("error") == "success":
                    out[names[rid - 1]] = m.get("data")
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        pass
    finally:
        s.close()
    return out

def volume_filter(db):
    """The filter chain for a gain in dB - and nothing at all for no gain, so
    a song that needs no moving is not run through a filter that does none."""
    return "volume=%.1fdB" % db if db else ""

class Mpv:
    """The mpv this player is driving.

    Every command is sent and forgotten: each one either takes effect or
    leaves playback as it was, and the loop is steered by mpv's events rather
    than by its replies, so there is never an answer worth waiting for.
    """

    def __init__(self):
        # A killed mpv leaves both its socket and itself behind, and the next
        # one can bind over neither. Asking it to quit is how it is disposed
        # of: there is no pid here worth trusting, and no need for one.
        quit_stale_mpv()
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(MPVSOCK), exist_ok=True)
        self.proc = subprocess.Popen(
            ["mpv"] + MPV_ARGS + ["--input-ipc-server=" + MPVSOCK],
            stdin=NULL, stdout=NULL, stderr=NULL)
        self.buf, self.n, self.dead = b"", 0, False
        self.sock = None
        for _ in range(100):    # the socket appears a moment after mpv does
            try:
                s = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX)
                s.connect(MPVSOCK)
                self.sock = s
                return
            except OSError:
                time.sleep(0.05)
        self.dead = True

    def send(self, *cmd):
        self.n += 1
        try:
            self.sock.sendall(json.dumps({"command": list(cmd),
                                          "request_id": self.n}).encode() + b"\n")
        except (OSError, AttributeError):   # gone, or never there to begin with
            self.dead = True

    def load(self, path, db, vol, start=0.0):
        """Start a song, at its beginning unless told otherwise. Unpausing
        first, so the file that lands is playing: pause is mpv's own state and
        outlives the song it was set on, and so is the volume - it is set here
        every time so that the two can never drift apart, whatever happened to
        the song before.

        `start` is where it opens, which is only ever moved by a song being
        swapped from the stream to the copy that has since landed on the disk.
        It is set before the file is loaded because that is when mpv reads it,
        and written every time - "none" when there is nowhere in particular to
        open - so a position can never be left behind for the next song."""
        self.send("set_property", "pause", False)
        self.send("set_property", "volume", vol)
        self.send("set_property", "af", volume_filter(db))
        self.send("set_property", "start", str(start) if start else "none")
        self.send("loadfile", path, "replace")

    def lines(self):
        """Whole JSON messages, as many as have arrived. An empty read is mpv
        hanging up, which is the one thing the player can't carry on past."""
        try:
            got = self.sock.recv(65536)
        except OSError:
            got = b""
        if not got:
            self.dead = True
            return []
        self.buf += got
        out = []
        while b"\n" in self.buf:
            line, self.buf = self.buf.split(b"\n", 1)
            if line.strip():
                try:
                    out.append(json.loads(line))
                except ValueError:
                    pass
        return out

    def until_end(self, wake, drain, tick=None, every=1.0):
        """Wait for the song to finish, and say how it finished - played out,
        stopped for a skip, or a file that turned out not to be playable. The
        player doesn't need mpv's word for what to do next (`step` already
        holds that, set by whoever asked) but it does need it for the log:
        "stop" and "eof" are the difference between a song you skipped and one
        you heard to the end, and nothing else here can tell them apart.

        `wake` is the pipe a command writes to, so a skip breaks the wait as
        promptly as an event does, and `drain` is what carries out whatever
        the handler asked for. Every command mpv gets is sent from here, in
        the ordinary flow - never from a handler, which could cut in halfway
        through a line being written and leave mpv something it can't read.

        `tick` runs on a quiet second and nowhere else: it reads where we are
        in the song, which mpv forgets the instant the song ends, so a skip
        has a position to be logged at. It is skipped whenever anything is
        actually ready, so a command is never made to wait behind a read."""
        while not self.dead:
            drain()
            try:
                ready = select.select([self.sock, wake], [], [], every)[0]
            except OSError:
                return "error"
            if not ready:
                if tick:
                    tick()
                continue
            if wake in ready:
                try:
                    os.read(wake, 4096)     # the byte itself says nothing; the
                except OSError:             # flag the handler set is the point
                    pass
            if self.sock in ready:
                for m in self.lines():
                    if m.get("event") == "end-file":
                        return m.get("reason") or "eof"
        return "error"                      # mpv hung up mid-song

    def kill(self):
        try:
            self.proc.kill()
        except OSError:
            pass

# ------------------------------------------------------------- the play log
# Every song that sounds is written down, one line per song, as it ends. The
# player is the only thing that writes here, because the player is the only
# thing that knows: a window polling what is playing would miss everything
# played from a terminal, count twice with two windows open, and could not
# tell a skip from a seek. One line covers the whole span a song was up for -
# how it was chosen, how far it got, and why it stopped - so a skip, a play
# and a song you walked back to are the same record read three ways.
#
# JSONL and append-only, on purpose. A line is one `write` to a file opened
# O_APPEND, which is atomic for anything this size, needs no read-modify-write
# and cannot be interrupted into a half-file - and it is written from a signal
# handler when the player is asked to stop, where anything that takes a lock
# could deadlock instead. A line that fails to be written is lost and nothing
# else is: the log is a record, and playback never waits on it.
#
# It lives in the music folder, hidden beside the playlists - see `plays_path`.
# Appending suits that too: two machines syncing a folder between them are
# each adding lines to the end of one file rather than rewriting it, which is
# the shape a sync has the best chance of reconciling.

# How the span ended. `eof` is mpv's own word for a song played out; the rest
# are ours, because mpv only ever says "stop" and cannot know who asked.
ENDS = ("eof", "skip", "back", "swap", "stop", "error")

def log_play(path, rec):
    """One finished span, appended. Never raises: a log is not worth a song.

    The file is named by the caller rather than looked up here, because the
    one caller that matters is closing a span from a signal handler on the way
    out - and reading the config to find where the music folder is, at that
    moment, is work that can be done earlier and cannot then fail late."""
    try:
        line = (json.dumps(rec, separators=(",", ":")) + "\n").encode()
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_APPEND, 0o600)
        try:
            os.write(fd, line)
        finally:
            os.close(fd)
    except (OSError, ValueError, TypeError):
        pass

def source_of(path):
    """Where the bytes came from: the network, the cache a stream is filling,
    or a file in your library."""
    if remote(path):
        return "stream"
    try:
        return "cache" if under(os.path.dirname(path or ""), cache_dir()) else "file"
    except OSError:
        return "file"

def open_span(job, live):
    """A song has started. Begin its record.

    `heard` is counted off the wall clock rather than off mpv's position,
    because the wall clock is what your ears did: a song seeked back through
    is heard twice over and a song paused is not heard at all, and neither of
    those reads off a position. The clock starts here and pauses subtract.

    A span belongs to the song, not to the file mpv is playing - a stream that
    lands on the disk mid-song is reloaded from the copy and keeps this one,
    which is why the loop only opens a span when it isn't resuming."""
    song = job.get("song")
    path = (job.get("paths") or {}).get(song) or ""
    live["rec"] = {"at": round(time.time(), 3), "song": song,
                   "playlist": job.get("playlist"), "pick": job.get("pick"),
                   "src": source_of(path), "vid": job.get("vid"),
                   "shuffle": bool(job.get("shuffle"))}
    # Where the line will go, settled now: the song is playing out of this
    # library, so it is this library's log it belongs in even if the folder is
    # changed under us while it plays.
    live["log"] = plays_path()
    live["t0"], live["held"], live["since"] = time.time(), 0.0, None
    live.pop("pos", None)
    live.pop("of", None)
    live.pop("seen", None)
    if job.get("paused"):       # swapped in mid-song while paused: not heard
        live["since"] = time.time()

def hold_span(live, paused):
    """Start or stop counting a pause against the span. Idempotent, because
    the pause command is sent as a repair as much as a command and arrives
    whether the flag moved or not."""
    now = time.time()
    if paused and live.get("since") is None:
        live["since"] = now
    elif not paused and live.get("since") is not None:
        live["held"] = live.get("held", 0.0) + now - live.pop("since")
        live["since"] = None
    # The sample clock starts again either way: a pause is counted here, and
    # the position standing still across one is the same pause seen twice.
    live.pop("seen", None)

def sample_span(live, pos):
    """Where in the song we are, taken on the tick. mpv drops the position the
    moment a file ends, so the last sample before the end is the only place a
    skip's position can come from - it is worth a second, and no more.

    The tick is also the only thing here that can tell playing from standing
    still, and `heard` is counted off the wall clock - so it has to. A song
    that is up but not moving is running that clock without reaching anybody:
    a stream stalled on the network, a laptop that slept with the music on,
    mpv paused by something that is not us. A sample that finds the position
    exactly where the last one left it is time held, not time heard - the
    same as a pause, and for the same reason. It is the difference between a
    four minute song and the hour one of them once claimed."""
    now = time.time()
    if pos.get("time-pos") is not None:
        was, seen = live.get("pos"), live.get("seen")
        live["pos"] = max(0.0, float(pos["time-pos"]))
        # A seek counts as heard, in either direction: the second it took is a
        # second the song was on, and only a position that has not moved at
        # all is a song that was not playing.
        if was is not None and seen is not None and abs(live["pos"] - was) < 0.05:
            live["held"] = live.get("held", 0.0) + max(0.0, now - seen)
        live["seen"] = now
    if pos.get("duration") is not None:
        live["of"] = round(float(pos["duration"]), 1)

def close_span(live, why):
    """The song is over. Write the line.

    Popped rather than read, so the two things that can close a span - the
    loop reaching the end of a song, and `finish` being called from the socket
    thread or a signal - cannot both write it."""
    rec = live.pop("rec", None)
    if not rec:
        return
    hold_span(live, False)              # a span closed while paused stops here
    of = live.get("of")
    # Played out means played to the end, whatever the last sample said: it is
    # up to a second old and the song is over by definition.
    reached = of if why == "eof" and of else live.get("pos")
    heard = max(0.0, time.time() - live.get("t0", time.time()) - live.get("held", 0.0))
    # `held` goes down beside `heard` because the two together are the only
    # way to say when the listening happened: a span covers the wall clock
    # from `at` to `at + heard + held`, and a chart of hours has to know that
    # much to put a song on either side of the hour it ran through.
    rec.update({"end": why if why in ENDS else "error",
                "reached": round(reached, 1) if reached is not None else None,
                "of": of, "heard": round(heard, 1),
                "held": round(max(0.0, live.get("held", 0.0)), 1)})
    log_play(live.get("log") or plays_path(), rec)

# --------------------------------------------------------------- the record
# The log, read back. Every span ever played is a line in it, and reading all
# of them is how every question here is answered: there is no running total
# kept anywhere, because a total is a second thing to keep in step with the
# lines and the lines are cheap to add up. A year of heavy listening is a few
# thousand of them, which is a few milliseconds and a megabyte.
#
# Nothing here trusts the file. It is appended to by every player that has
# ever run against this library - two machines syncing the folder between them
# are both writing to it - so a line can arrive torn, or twice, or out of the
# order it happened in, and not one of those is worth losing the history over.
# A line that cannot be read is skipped and the rest are still counted.

# How a span ended, sorted into what it says about the song. `eof` is the song
# played out. The three actives are you cutting it short yourself: `skip` and
# `back` are the buttons that mean "not this one", and `swap` is you going and
# fetching something else instead - which says more about what you wanted than
# about what was on. `stop` and `error` say nothing either way: the song
# playing when you quit for the evening never got a verdict, and counting that
# as a skip would punish it for having been last.
ACTIVE = ("skip", "back", "swap")
NEUTRAL = ("stop", "error")
JUDGED = ("eof",) + ACTIVE          # spans you expressed something about

SESSION_GAP = 1800      # half an hour of silence is the end of an evening

def num(v):
    """A number off a logged line, or None if it isn't one. The file is shared
    between machines and versions, so a field is only ever what it looks
    like - and a stats page is not worth crashing over a bad one."""
    try:
        v = float(v)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None
    return v if v == v and v not in (float("inf"), float("-inf")) else None

def read_plays(path=None, since=None):
    """Every span, oldest first, with the damage stepped over.

    Sorted rather than taken as it comes: one player appends in the order it
    played, but two machines syncing one folder interleave, and anything that
    reads a sequence out of this - an evening, a run of skips - would read a
    shuffled one otherwise.

    Deduplicated on the moment and the song, which together name a span: a
    sync that resolves a conflict by keeping both copies would otherwise have
    you listening to the same evening twice."""
    try:
        with open(path or plays_path(), "r", encoding="utf-8",
                  errors="replace") as f:
            lines = f.readlines()
    except OSError:
        return []                   # no log yet is not an error, it is Tuesday
    rows, seen = [], set()
    for line in lines:
        line = line.strip()
        if not line:
            continue
        try:
            r = json.loads(line)
        except ValueError:
            continue                # a torn line, or half a file a sync landed
        at = num(isinstance(r, dict) and r.get("at"))
        if at is None or not isinstance(r.get("song"), str) or not r["song"]:
            continue
        key = (round(at, 3), r["song"])
        if key in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(key)
        if since is not None and at < since:
            continue
        r["at"], r["heard"] = at, max(num(r.get("heard")) or 0.0, 0.0)
        # Lines written before pauses were recorded have no `held`, which
        # reads as a span that ran straight through - which is what it was,
        # give or take the pause nobody wrote down.
        r["held"] = max(num(r.get("held")) or 0.0, 0.0)
        r["reached"], r["of"] = num(r.get("reached")), num(r.get("of"))
        # A song cannot have been heard for longer than it lasts. The player
        # no longer writes lines that say otherwise, but the log is older than
        # that and is appended to by every version that ever ran against this
        # library - and one span of a four minute song claiming an hour is all
        # it takes for an hour of the chart to report sixty-two minutes. The
        # excess becomes time the song was up and not heard, so the stretch of
        # clock the span covers is still the stretch it really covered.
        if r["of"] and r["heard"] > r["of"]:
            r["held"] += r["heard"] - r["of"]
            r["heard"] = r["of"]
        r["end"] = r.get("end") if r.get("end") in ENDS else "error"
        rows.append(r)
    rows.sort(key=lambda r: r["at"])
    return rows

def span_of(rec):
    """The stretch of wall clock one span covered: from the moment the song
    came on to the moment it stopped being on. Listening plus any time it
    stood paused, because a pause is time the song was still up - it just
    wasn't heard.

    This is the only thing that knows a song has length. Everything that
    places listening in time goes through here, so an evening and an hour of
    the chart can never disagree about when a song was on."""
    return rec["at"], rec["at"] + rec["heard"] + rec.get("held", 0.0)

def pool_of(rec):
    """Which list a span was played out of. The library is one of them - it is
    a pool like any other here, and calling it by the name the window and
    `list` both use it by is what keeps one answer from becoming two."""
    return rec.get("playlist") or LIBRARY_NAME

def spell_secs(secs):
    """Seconds as somebody would say them out loud. The window says it the
    same way; this is the one place the wording is decided."""
    secs = int(round(secs or 0))
    if secs < 90:
        return "%d sec" % secs
    mins = int(round(secs / 60.0))
    if mins < 90:
        return "%d min" % mins
    return "%d hr %d min" % (mins // 60, mins % 60)

def pct(part, whole):
    """A percentage, or None when there is nothing to take one of. None is not
    zero: a song you have never cut short has no skip rate, and printing 0%
    would claim we asked and the answer was none."""
    return round(100.0 * part / whole, 1) if whole else None

def bail_bucket(rec):
    """How far into a song you left it, in the four sizes that mean different
    things. Inside five seconds is not liking the song; past halfway is liking
    it and having somewhere else to be. Lumping those together as one 'skip'
    is what loses the only interesting thing the position knows."""
    at, of = rec.get("reached"), rec.get("of")
    if at is None:
        return "unknown"            # a stream cut before mpv said how long
    if at < 5:
        return "first 5 sec"
    if at < 30:
        return "first 30 sec"
    if not of or at < of / 2.0:
        return "first half"
    return "past halfway"

BAILS = ("first 5 sec", "first 30 sec", "first half", "past halfway", "unknown")

def counted(rows, field):
    """One field's values, tallied, biggest first."""
    out = {}
    for r in rows:
        v = r.get(field)
        v = v if isinstance(v, str) else str(v)
        out[v] = out.get(v, 0) + 1
    return dict(sorted(out.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]))

def group(rows, key):
    """Spans gathered under whatever names them - a song, or a playlist - and
    each group added up the same way. One function for both, so `stats songs`
    and `stats playlists` can never come to different arithmetic."""
    out = {}
    for r in rows:
        name = key(r)
        g = out.get(name)
        if not g:
            g = out[name] = {"name": name, "heard": 0.0, "plays": 0,
                             "finished": 0, "active": 0, "skipped": 0,
                             "swapped": 0, "backed": 0, "chosen": 0,
                             "drawn": 0, "first": r["at"], "last": r["at"],
                             "songs": set()}
        g["heard"] += r["heard"]
        g["plays"] += 1
        g["last"] = max(g["last"], r["at"])
        g["first"] = min(g["first"], r["at"])
        g["songs"].add(r["song"])
        end = r["end"]
        g["finished"] += end == "eof"
        g["active"] += end in ACTIVE
        g["skipped"] += end == "skip"
        g["swapped"] += end == "swap"
        g["backed"] += end == "back"
        g["chosen"] += r.get("pick") == "you"
        g["drawn"] += r.get("pick") == "draw"
    for g in out.values():
        judged = g["finished"] + g["active"]
        g["judged"] = judged
        g["finish_rate"] = pct(g["finished"], judged)
        g["skip_rate"] = pct(g["active"], judged)
        g["songs"] = len(g["songs"])
        g["heard"] = round(g["heard"], 1)
    return list(out.values())

# How a table of groups can be ordered. Time listened is the default and the
# honest one: a play is a song that started, and four aborted starts hunting
# for the right version of a track will out-count an evening spent inside one
# song. Both are here because the count is the one everybody looks for, and
# seeing it beside the time is what shows why it is the wrong column.
ORDERS = {"time": lambda g: (-g["heard"], -g["plays"], g["name"].lower()),
          "plays": lambda g: (-g["plays"], -g["heard"], g["name"].lower()),
          "skips": lambda g: (-g["active"], -g["plays"], g["name"].lower()),
          "recent": lambda g: (-g["last"], g["name"].lower())}

def rank(groups, by="time", count=None):
    rows = sorted(groups, key=ORDERS.get(by, ORDERS["time"]))
    return rows[:count] if count else rows

def sessions(rows):
    """The evenings. A gap of half an hour with nothing playing ends one -
    long enough that a cup of tea between songs doesn't split an evening in
    two, short enough that tonight is never counted as part of yesterday."""
    out = []
    for r in rows:
        if out and r["at"] - out[-1]["end"] <= SESSION_GAP:
            here = out[-1]
        else:
            here = {"at": r["at"], "end": r["at"], "heard": 0.0, "plays": 0}
            out.append(here)
        # The span ended after it was heard, not when it started: an evening
        # runs to the end of its last song and not to the beginning of it.
        here["end"] = max(here["end"], span_of(r)[1])
        here["heard"] += r["heard"]
        here["plays"] += 1
    for s in out:
        s["heard"] = round(s["heard"], 1)
        s["length"] = round(s["end"] - s["at"], 1)
    return out

# Listening time over time, at three sizes. The same series each way: an hour
# of the day, a day, a week - so switching between them is a zoom rather than
# a different question, and the window can hold all three and switch without
# asking again.
STEPS = {"hour": (3600, 48), "day": (86400, 30), "week": (604800, 26)}

def bucket_start(at, step):
    """The moment the bucket holding `at` begins, in local time - because a day
    is a local thing and a log that has crossed a timezone still happened in
    the evenings it happened in.

    Every step back is taken from the middle of a bucket rather than its edge,
    which is what keeps the hour the clocks change from landing in the bucket
    next door."""
    t = time.localtime(at)
    day = (t.tm_year, t.tm_mon, t.tm_mday)
    if step == "hour":
        return time.mktime(day + (t.tm_hour, 0, 0, 0, 0, -1))
    if step == "week":                  # back to the Monday it belongs to
        back = time.localtime(time.mktime(day + (12, 0, 0, 0, 0, -1))
                              - t.tm_wday * 86400)
        day = (back.tm_year, back.tm_mon, back.tm_mday)
    return time.mktime(day + (0, 0, 0, 0, 0, -1))

def next_bucket(b, step, span):
    """The bucket after this one, and how the length of a bucket is known.
    Taken from the middle of it rather than its edge, the same way the walk
    back is, so the day the clocks change is a step of 23 or 25 hours and
    still lands exactly one bucket along."""
    nxt = bucket_start(b + span * 1.5, step)
    return nxt if nxt > b else b + span      # never stand still, whatever the clock says

def pour(into, rec, step, span):
    """One span's listening time, added to the buckets it was heard in.

    A song is not heard at the instant it starts, it is heard over the minutes
    that follow: the five minutes of a song put on at 10:58 are two minutes of
    the ten o'clock hour and three of the eleven, not five of the ten. Charged
    whole to the hour it started in - which is what this used to do - an hour
    with a song running through each of its ends could report more listening
    than an hour holds, and 62 minutes in an hour is the one number a chart of
    hours must never be able to show.

    Split by how much of the span each bucket holds. Where a song stood paused
    there is no telling which side of the boundary the pause fell, so the
    listening is spread evenly across the time the song was up - exact for
    every span that ran straight through, and the honest guess for the rest."""
    at, end = span_of(rec)
    if rec["heard"] <= 0:
        return
    b, wall = bucket_start(at, step), end - at
    if wall <= 0:                   # a span with no length is a moment, not a stretch
        into[b] = into.get(b, 0.0) + rec["heard"]
        return
    while b < end:
        nxt = next_bucket(b, step, span)
        lo, hi = max(at, b), min(end, nxt)
        if hi > lo:
            into[b] = into.get(b, 0.0) + rec["heard"] * (hi - lo) / wall
        b = nxt

def series(rows, step):
    """How long you listened in each of the last buckets, oldest first.

    The empty ones are kept. A quiet Tuesday is part of the shape of a week,
    and a chart that closed the gap up would draw a month of listening as if
    it had all been one - which is the one thing this is meant to show."""
    span, count = STEPS[step]
    into = {}
    for r in rows:
        pour(into, r, step, span)
    at, marks = bucket_start(time.time(), step), []
    for _ in range(count):
        marks.append(at)
        at = bucket_start(at - span / 2.0, step)     # a step back, DST and all
    # A bucket cannot hold more listening than it has time in it. One span can
    # no longer overfill one, but two players against the same library overlap
    # in real time and their lines both land here, so the ceiling is kept as a
    # ceiling rather than left to arithmetic that no longer needs it.
    return [{"at": b, "heard": round(min(into.get(b, 0.0),
                                         next_bucket(b, step, span) - b), 1),
             "label": time.strftime({"hour": "%H:%M", "week": "%-d %b"}
                                    .get(step, "%a %-d"), time.localtime(b)),
             "full": time.strftime({"hour": "%a %-d %b, %H:%M"}
                                   .get(step, "%a %-d %b"), time.localtime(b))}
            for b in reversed(marks)]

def stats(rows):
    """Everything the command reports, worked out in one pass over the log.

    One shape, whichever topic asked: the text views each print a slice of
    this and `--json` prints all of it, so the window and the terminal are
    reading the same arithmetic rather than each doing its own."""
    judged = [r for r in rows if r["end"] in JUDGED]
    active = [r for r in judged if r["end"] in ACTIVE]
    finished = [r for r in judged if r["end"] == "eof"]
    fracs = sorted(r["reached"] / r["of"] for r in active
                   if r["reached"] is not None and r["of"])
    reached = sorted(r["reached"] for r in active if r["reached"] is not None)
    bail = {b: 0 for b in BAILS}
    for r in active:
        bail[bail_bucket(r)] += 1
    every = sessions(rows)
    clock = {"%02d" % h: 0 for h in range(24)}
    for r in rows:
        clock["%02d" % time.localtime(r["at"]).tm_hour] += 1
    return {
        "spans": len(rows),
        "songs": len({r["song"] for r in rows}),
        "heard": round(sum(r["heard"] for r in rows), 1),
        "first": rows[0]["at"] if rows else None,
        "last": rows[-1]["at"] if rows else None,
        "finished": len(finished),
        "judged": len(judged),
        "finish_rate": pct(len(finished), len(judged)),
        "active": len(active),
        "skip_rate": pct(len(active), len(judged)),
        "skipped": sum(1 for r in active if r["end"] == "skip"),
        "swapped": sum(1 for r in active if r["end"] == "swap"),
        "backed": sum(1 for r in active if r["end"] == "back"),
        "neutral": sum(1 for r in rows if r["end"] in NEUTRAL),
        "chosen": sum(1 for r in rows if r.get("pick") == "you"),
        "drawn": sum(1 for r in rows if r.get("pick") == "draw"),
        "bail_median_frac": round(100 * fracs[len(fracs) // 2], 1) if fracs else None,
        "bail_median_secs": round(reached[len(reached) // 2], 1) if reached else None,
        "bail": bail,
        "ends": counted(rows, "end"),
        "sources": counted(rows, "src"),
        "clock": clock,
        "series": {k: series(rows, k) for k in STEPS},
        "sessions": len(every),
        "longest_session": round(max((s["heard"] for s in every), default=0.0), 1),
        "by_song": group(rows, lambda r: r["song"]),
        "by_playlist": group(rows, pool_of),
        "recent": every[-1] if every else None,
    }

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- player

def window_of(songs):
    """How far back "lately" reaches: half the pool. Two songs means one, which
    is the song you are on - so the other is always fair game, and a pair still
    alternates instead of seizing up."""
    return max(1, min(len(songs) - 1, len(songs) // 2))

def draw(songs, plays, heard=()):
    """The shuffle draw, in two steps that cannot fight each other.

    First the hard rule: nothing heard in the last half a playlist, however it
    came to be heard. That one is absolute - a song you clicked counts as
    heard just as much as one the draw chose, because your ears cannot tell
    the difference and the whole point is not to hear it again so soon.

    Then, of what is left, the songs the playlist has played fewest, and
    chance between those. So a playlist still comes out even round by round,
    and the levelling decides the order within what the window allows.

    They cannot deadlock in a real playlist. j songs into a round, the fewest-
    played pool holds N-j songs while the window can bar at most N//2 - j of
    them, and N-j is always the larger. Only a one-song playlist runs out, and
    then the fallbacks hand back the one song there is."""
    recent = set(heard[-window_of(songs):])
    fresh = ([s for s in songs if s not in recent]
             or [s for s in songs if s != (heard[-1] if heard else None)]
             or list(songs))
    fewest = min(plays.get(s, 0) for s in fresh)
    return random.choice([s for s in fresh if plays.get(s, 0) == fewest])

def pool_key(job):
    """Which tally a queue counts into. A count is a playlist's, not a song's:
    the same song in two playlists is two different things owed, because it is
    the playlist that has to come out even. The library is a pool like any
    other and keeps its own, under a name no playlist can have."""
    return job.get("playlist") or ""

def tally(job):
    """This queue's counts: what its playlist has heard so far this session."""
    return (job.get("plays") or {}).get(pool_key(job)) or {}

def heard_in(job):
    """What this queue's playlist has heard lately, oldest first."""
    return (job.get("heard") or {}).get(pool_key(job)) or []

def note_heard(job, song):
    """Remember that a song has just been heard - every song that starts, and
    it does not matter at all who chose it.

    This is not the tally beside it. `plays` is what the draw owes each song,
    so only the queue's own choices move it; `heard` is what came out of the
    speakers, so everything does. Keeping them apart is what lets a song you
    clicked be barred from coming round again without also being counted as a
    play the shuffle owed you.

    A song already in the line moves to the end of it rather than sitting
    twice, and the line is trimmed to the pool: no window reaches further."""
    heard, key = job.get("heard") or {}, pool_key(job)
    line = [s for s in heard_in(job) if s != song] + [song]
    job["heard"] = dict(heard, **{key: line[-max(1, len(job.get("songs") or [1])):]})

def count_play(job, song):
    """Count a play into this queue's playlist, and hand the song back.

    What counts is the queue choosing: the draw, and the song a playlist opens
    on. What doesn't is you choosing mid-playlist - click your way around all
    evening and the counts do not move, so what the draw owes each song is
    unchanged when you leave it to the draw again."""
    plays, key, was = job.get("plays") or {}, pool_key(job), tally(job)
    counted = dict(was, **{song: was.get(song, 0) + 1})
    # New dicts rather than changed ones, at both levels: the socket thread
    # reads this while the playing loop counts, and swapping the whole thing
    # over means it can only ever see one tally or the next, never one being
    # written into.
    job["plays"] = dict(plays, **{key: counted})
    return song

def credit_start(job):
    """The song a queue opens on, counted unless it is you moving about inside
    a playlist that is already going.

    Two ways it counts. It was drawn, which is the shuffle choosing. Or it is
    the first thing this playlist has played all session - putting a playlist
    on is starting it, and the song you start it on is a song you have heard,
    whether you named it or the draw did. What is left is the case that must
    not count: playlist already running, and you pick a song out of it.

    `play` does the drawing itself, because it is what tells you which song it
    has started - but the counting belongs here, with the session, which is the
    only place that knows what a playlist has heard already."""
    drawn = job.pop("drawn", False)
    # How the opening song was chosen, which the log wants and the tally does
    # not: the draw picked it, or you did - `play SONG`, a row clicked in the
    # window, a search streamed. The two questions have different answers, and
    # this is the one that doesn't care whether the playlist was already going.
    job["pick"] = "draw" if drawn else "you"
    opening = pool_key(job) not in (job.get("plays") or {})
    if (drawn or opening) and job.get("order"):
        count_play(job, job["order"][0])

def draw_next(job):
    """The next song when shuffling: the draw, over the pool we are playing,
    counted because we are the one choosing. The song just finished is the
    last thing heard, so the window keeps it out without being told to."""
    return count_play(job, draw(job["songs"], tally(job), heard_in(job)))

def advance(job, step):
    """Move one song. Going back walks `order`, which is the history, so it
    replays exactly what you heard; going forward off the end of it appends
    whatever comes next. Nothing appended means the queue is finished."""
    # Walking history, in either direction, until it runs out and the draw or
    # the list decides - which is the one case the log calls a draw.
    job["pick"] = "back" if step < 0 else "next"
    job["index"] = max(0, job["index"] + step)   # back at the start replays
    if job["index"] < len(job["order"]):
        return
    if job["shuffle"]:
        job["order"].append(draw_next(job))
        job["pick"] = "draw"
    else:
        i = job["songs"].index(job["order"][-1]) + 1 \
            if job["order"][-1] in job["songs"] else len(job["songs"])
        if i < len(job["songs"]):
            job["order"].append(job["songs"][i])

def follow_on(job, guard):
    """Carry a finished stream on into the list its song was kept in, turning
    the job into that queue, and say whether there was one to turn it into.

    A stream is a queue of one, so the end of it used to be the end of the
    music - even when, while it played, you had just told this app where the
    song lives. Being told that is the whole answer to what comes next: the
    playlist you filed it in, the newest of them if you filed it in more than
    one, and your library if you only kept it. Keeping it nowhere is the one
    case with no answer, and that one stops the way every stream used to.

    False means there is nowhere to go, and the player is about to stop - so
    what state the job is left in is nobody's business by then."""
    song = job["song"]
    stem = filed_under(song)
    if stem:
        listed = songs_of(stem)
    elif song in index():
        listed = [{"file": n, "path": p} for n, p in index().items()]
    else:
        return False                # kept nowhere: nowhere to carry on into
    paths = {p["file"]: p["path"] for p in listed if p["path"]}
    if not paths:
        return False                # a list, but nothing in it is on the disk
    shuffle = shuffling()

    # Read off the disk above and written here, because the socket thread
    # answers out of `job`: it has to see the queue this was or the queue it
    # became, and never one turning into the other.
    with guard:
        was = pool_key(job)
        job["playlist"], job["songs"] = stem, list(paths)
        job["paths"], job["shuffle"] = paths, shuffle
        job.pop("vid", None)    # on the disk now: nothing left to fetch for it
        if pool_key(job) != was:
            # A play and a hearing, counted into the list the song has just
            # joined, because the draw over that list has to know it as a song
            # this playlist has played - or the first thing it does is hand
            # straight back the song you have this second finished listening
            # to. Only when the pool changes: the library is the pool a stream
            # already counts into, and counting it there again is twice.
            note_heard(job, count_play(job, song))
        if song in paths:
            # What just played is this queue's history rather than its
            # opening: in `order`, so `back` replays what you actually heard,
            # and behind `advance`, so what follows it is picked exactly the
            # way it would be for any other song in this list - the draw when
            # shuffling, and the next row down when not.
            job["order"], job["index"] = [song], 0
            advance(job, 1)
        else:
            # In the list, but the copy is still coming down - so there is
            # nothing to play it from and nothing to carry on from. The list
            # opens instead, the way `play` would have opened it.
            job["order"], job["index"] = [draw_next(job) if shuffle
                                          else job["songs"][0]], 0
            job["pick"] = "draw" if shuffle else "next"
        if job["index"] >= len(job["order"]):
            return False        # the last row of a list played in order
    if stem:
        mark_played(stem)       # it is the list you are playing now
    return True

def local_copy(job, song):
    """A copy of this song on the disk, or None while the network is still the
    only way to hear it. The library first, then the cache being filled behind
    a song being streamed - and that one only once it is whole, which is what
    the note beside it says. A half-written file is not a copy."""
    have = index().get(song)
    if have:
        return have
    vid = job.get("vid")
    if vid and cached_ready(vid) == song:
        return os.path.join(cache_dir(), song)
    return None

def serve_control(job, want, guard, wake_w, finish):
    """The player answering for itself, on its own thread.

    A thread rather than a turn of the playing loop, because the loop spends
    real time in places it cannot be interrupted - measuring a song's loudness
    is a decode pass, and a window polling through that has to be told what is
    playing rather than left hanging until the song starts.

    It never writes to mpv. It leaves a note in `want` and prods the loop
    awake, exactly as the signal handlers it replaces did, so every command
    mpv is given is still written by the one thread that writes to it and no
    two commands can be interleaved halfway through a line.

    The exceptions are the two that must happen whether the loop is listening
    or not: reading how far into the song we are, which asks mpv on a
    connection of its own, and stopping, which is the end of the process."""

    def nudge(**note):
        with guard:
            want.update(note)
        try:
            os.write(wake_w, b".")      # break the loop out of its wait
        except OSError:
            pass

    def snapshot():
        """Everything anyone asks about what is playing, in one answer. It is
        one answer on purpose: a caller that got the song from here and the
        position from somewhere else could be handed two different moments."""
        with guard:
            song, songs = job.get("song"), job.get("songs") or []
            out = {"playing": True, "ok": True, "song": song,
                   "path": (job.get("paths") or {}).get(song),
                   "playlist": job.get("playlist"), "index": job.get("index", 0),
                   "queue": len(songs), "paused": bool(job.get("paused")),
                   "shuffle": bool(job.get("shuffle")), "seq": job.get("seq", 0),
                   "vid": job.get("vid"),
                   # Where we are in the playlist, which is not where we are in
                   # the session: `index` counts songs played, and a shuffled
                   # stretch runs it on past the end of the list.
                   "track": songs.index(song) + 1 if song in songs else None}
        # Outside the lock: mpv is asked over a socket with a timeout on it,
        # and the playing loop must never wait on that to change a song.
        pos = mpv_props("time-pos", "duration")
        out["elapsed"] = max(0.0, pos.get("time-pos") or 0.0)
        out["duration"] = pos.get("duration")
        return out

    def answer(req):
        op = req.get("op")
        if op == "now":
            return snapshot()
        if op == "plays":               # the session's counts and its recent
            with guard:                 # songs, for the one caller that needs
                return {"ok": True,     # them: `play`, drawing what to open on
                        "plays": dict(job.get("plays") or {}),
                        "heard": dict(job.get("heard") or {})}
        if op == "play":                # a new queue for the player we already
            new = req.get("job")        # have, rather than a second player
            if not new:
                return {"ok": False, "error": "no queue"}
            with guard:
                job["ended_by"] = "swap"    # mpv will say "stop"; only we know
            nudge(swap=new, stop=True)      # it was another queue being put on
            return {"ok": True, "song": new.get("song")}
        if op == "stop":
            return None                 # answered, then acted on: see below
        if op in ("skip", "back"):
            with guard:
                job["step"] = 1 if op == "skip" else -1
                job["ended_by"] = op        # the song did not end, you ended it
            nudge(stop=True)
            return {"ok": True}
        if op == "pause":
            # `job["paused"]` is the whole truth about whether we are paused,
            # and this is the only place anything outside sets it. The note
            # left for the loop carries no value of its own - it says "mpv
            # disagrees with the flag", so the loop reads the flag when it gets
            # there rather than acting on a copy taken now. It is sent whether
            # the flag moved or not, which makes it a repair as much as a
            # command: any disagreement is settled by the next pause either way.
            with guard:
                was = bool(job.get("paused"))
                job["paused"] = (not was if req.get("want") is None
                                 else bool(req["want"]))
                paused, song = job["paused"], job.get("song")
            nudge(pause=True)
            # `changed` is what lets a caller say "already paused" honestly
            # instead of claiming an act it didn't perform.
            return {"ok": True, "paused": paused, "changed": paused != was,
                    "song": song}
        if op == "seek":
            nudge(seek=max(0.0, float(req.get("pos") or 0)))
            return {"ok": True}
        if op == "shuffle":
            with guard:
                job["shuffle"] = bool(req.get("on"))
                del job["order"][job["index"] + 1:]   # drop the redo history,
            nudge()                                   # so the new mode decides
            return {"ok": True}
        if op == "levelling":
            nudge(level=True)
            return {"ok": True}
        if op == "volume":
            nudge(volume=True)
            return {"ok": True}
        return {"ok": False, "error": "no such command: %s" % op}

    def handle(conn):
        conn.settimeout(5.0)
        buf = b""
        while b"\n" not in buf:
            got = conn.recv(65536)
            if not got:
                return
            buf += got
        req = json.loads(buf.split(b"\n", 1)[0])
        stopping = req.get("op") == "stop"
        reply = {"ok": True, "stopped": True} if stopping else answer(req)
        conn.sendall(json.dumps(reply).encode() + b"\n")
        if stopping:
            conn.close()        # answered before we go, so `stop` isn't a
            finish()            # question that dies with the process

    def loop(srv):
        while True:
            try:
                conn, _ = srv.accept()
            except OSError:
                return
            try:
                handle(conn)
            except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
                pass            # one bad caller is not the end of playback
            finally:
                try:
                    conn.close()
                except OSError:
                    pass

    # Only the lock holder gets here, so a socket file in the way is one left
    # behind by a player that is gone, and taking it is ours to do.
    try:
        os.remove(SOCK)
    except OSError:
        pass
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(SOCK), exist_ok=True)
    srv = socket.socket(socket.AF_UNIX)
    srv.bind(SOCK)
    srv.listen(16)
    threading.Thread(target=loop, args=(srv,), daemon=True).start()
    return srv

def player(job):
    """The detached process that owns playback: play one song, record it,
    repeat. It owns the mpv too - one, for the whole session - so the loop is
    "hand mpv a song, wait for it to end" rather than a player per song.

    It also *is* what is playing, as far as the rest of this app is concerned:
    it holds the lock that says so and answers the socket that says what. It
    outlives the window on purpose, so `simplmusik play` in a terminal and a
    window opened an hour later are the same playback - the window finds this
    process and asks it, rather than starting anything."""
    # Before anything else, and before a single sound: either we are the
    # player or there already is one. There is no third answer, and no way for
    # two processes to get past this line at once.
    if not hold_lock():
        # Somebody else has it. Hand them the queue we were started with - the
        # person asked for it either way - and leave. This is the race that
        # used to end with two players: it now ends with one, playing what was
        # asked for last.
        for _ in range(40):
            if send("play", job=job):
                return 0
            time.sleep(0.05)
        return 1

    mpv = None
    # What a command has asked for, carried out by `drain` in the loop below.
    # The socket thread only ever leaves a note here, because the loop is the
    # one thing that writes to mpv; see `Mpv.until_end`.
    want, guard = {}, threading.Lock()
    # A command breaks the loop's `select` by writing to this pipe. Without it
    # a note would be left and the loop would go straight back to waiting on
    # mpv, and a skip would not be acted on until the song ended.
    wake_r, wake_w = os.pipe()
    for fd in (wake_r, wake_w):
        os.set_blocking(fd, False)
    # The song sounding now, as the log will have it. It is here rather than
    # on the job because a swap clears the job, and the song the old queue was
    # playing still has a line to be written about it.
    live = {}

    def finish(code=0):
        """Stop being the player, and stop being a process. The lock goes when
        this exits, which is the only thing that has to happen and the one
        thing we cannot forget to do."""
        # The song that was playing when you stopped is still a song you
        # heard, and this is the last moment anything knows about it. Reached
        # from a signal and from the socket thread as well as from the loop,
        # so it is written with one `write` and no lock: see `log_play`.
        close_span(live, "stop")
        if mpv:
            mpv.kill()
        for path in [SOCK, MPVSOCK] + LEGACY:
            try:
                os.remove(path)     # ours to remove: we hold the lock
            except OSError:
                pass
        os._exit(code)

    # Counted before anything can ask, because the session starts here: the
    # song we were handed is the first play of it, if a draw is what chose it.
    credit_start(job)
    # Answering starts before mpv does, so a `play` that has only just
    # returned is already something `status` and the window can ask about.
    serve_control(job, want, guard, wake_w, finish)
    # Killed rather than asked - the sandbox torn down around us, or a plain
    # `kill` - and there is no loop left to hand it to.
    for sig in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGINT):
        signal.signal(sig, lambda *_a: finish())

    mpv = Mpv()

    def drain():
        """Carry out what the socket thread asked for. Everything here is a
        write to mpv, which is why none of it happens on that thread."""
        with guard:
            todo, keep = {}, {}
            for k, v in want.items():
                (keep if k in ("swap", "restart") else todo)[k] = v
            want.clear()
            want.update(keep)
        if todo.get("pause"):
            # Read here, not where the command was taken: a song can end in
            # between, and ending one lands us playing the next. The flag knows
            # that and a value carried over from before it would not - which is
            # how mpv and the flag used to end up disagreeing, leaving playback
            # silent while everything that asked was told it was playing.
            with guard:
                paused = bool(job["paused"])
            hold_span(live, paused)     # paused time is not time you heard it
            mpv.send("set_property", "pause", paused)
        if "seek" in todo:
            at = todo["seek"]
            local = local_copy(job, job["song"])
            if local and remote(job["paths"][job["song"]]):
                # It finished downloading while you listened. A seek is the one
                # moment worth changing over on: you have asked for a jump
                # either way, so the gap it costs is one you were going to hear
                # anyway. Left alone, a stream plays out as a stream - this is
                # the only thing that ever swaps a song under itself.
                job["paths"][job["song"]] = local
                with guard:
                    want["restart"] = at
                mpv.send("stop")        # the loop below picks the song up again
            else:
                # The song carries on from there rather than starting again, so
                # there is nothing to put back afterwards: no offset to
                # remember, and a seek while paused is still paused when it
                # lands.
                mpv.send("seek", at, "absolute")
        if todo.get("level"):
            # Levelling turned on or off mid-song, or aimed somewhere else:
            # swap the filter under the song that is playing, so it is heard
            # now and heard seamlessly. The gain is worked out again from the
            # cache, so neither of those re-measures anything.
            job["gain"] = gain(job["song"], job["paths"][job["song"]])
            mpv.send("set_property", "af", volume_filter(job["gain"]))
        if todo.get("volume"):
            # mpv's own volume, left alone by everything else here, so turning
            # it down never disturbs the filter levelling is riding on.
            mpv.send("set_property", "volume", volume())
        if todo.get("stop"):
            mpv.send("stop")

    def tick():
        """A quiet second. Read where we are in the song, because mpv forgets
        it the instant the song ends and a skip has to be logged somewhere."""
        if live.get("rec") and not job.get("paused"):
            sample_span(live, mpv_props("time-pos", "duration"))

    at, resuming = 0.0, False
    while not mpv.dead:
        # A new queue, handed to us by whoever asked for it - from the window,
        # from a terminal, or by a player that lost the race to be us. It is
        # taken here, before a song is loaded, so nothing from the old queue is
        # ever heard after the new one was asked for.
        with guard:
            swap = want.pop("swap", None)
            if swap is not None:
                seq, plays = job.get("seq", 0), job.get("plays") or {}
                heard = job.get("heard") or {}
                job.clear()
                job.update(swap)
                job["seq"] = seq        # keeps counting: a caller watching for
                at, resuming = 0.0, False   # the song to change still sees it
                # The counts are the session's, not the queue's: press play on
                # another playlist and this one is still owed what it was owed
                # if you come back to it before the music stops. What it has
                # heard lately stays with it for the same reason - a new queue
                # on the same playlist is not a reason to hear a song again.
                job["plays"], job["heard"] = plays, heard
                credit_start(job)
                # Whatever was asked of the queue we have just put down does
                # not carry over to the one we are picking up: the stop that
                # came with the swap is the swap, and a seek was into a song
                # nobody is playing any more.
                want.pop("stop", None)
                want.pop("seek", None)
        if not 0 <= job["index"] < len(job["order"]):
            # A stream is a queue of one, and running out of it used to be the
            # end of the music. Now it is a question - where did you put that
            # song while it played? - and the list that answers plays on from
            # here, seamlessly, because it is this same player and this same
            # mpv picking up the next song. Nothing but a stream has anywhere
            # to go, and a stream that was kept nowhere hasn't either.
            if not (job.get("vid") and follow_on(job, guard)):
                break                   # queue finished
        job["song"] = job["order"][job["index"]]
        # Heard is heard: this is the one place a song actually starts, so it
        # is the one place the window is told about, and it takes draws, songs
        # you clicked and songs you walked back to without distinguishing.
        note_heard(job, job["song"])
        # ...and the one place a span opens, for the same reason. Not when
        # resuming: a stream that landed on the disk is reloaded from the copy
        # at the point you were at, which is the same song still playing.
        if not resuming:
            open_span(job, live)
        path = job["paths"][job["song"]]
        if not resuming:
            # A song picked back up off the disk keeps the gain it was already
            # playing at. Measuring the file costs a decode pass, which would
            # stall the seek that asked for it - and would move the loudness
            # halfway through a song. It is measured the next time it is
            # played, by which point it is an ordinary library song.
            job["gain"] = gain(job["song"], path)
        job["seq"] += 1         # every start, so a replayed song still counts
        mpv.load(path, job["gain"], volume(), at)
        if job["paused"]:       # swapped mid-song while paused: still paused
            mpv.send("set_property", "pause", True)
        reason = mpv.until_end(wake_r, drain, tick)
        # A swap ends the file mpv was playing, which is not the song ending:
        # the same song goes round again, off the disk, at the point asked for.
        with guard:
            at = want.pop("restart", None)
            pending = "swap" in want
            # Left where it is if we are only resuming: nothing has ended, so
            # whoever set it is still waiting for the ending they asked for.
            why = None if at is not None else job.pop("ended_by", None)
        resuming = at is not None
        if resuming:
            continue            # same song, same span: nothing has ended
        # mpv only ever says that it stopped; who stopped it, and why, is
        # known here and nowhere else. A song nobody interrupted ended by
        # itself, and mpv's own word for that is the one worth keeping.
        close_span(live, why or ("eof" if reason == "eof" else "error"))
        if pending:
            continue            # a queue waiting to be taken decides, not us
        at = 0.0
        # Both of these were asked of the song that has just ended, so neither
        # outlives it: a skip in the gap between two songs is already the move
        # the loop is making, and a seek into a song nobody is playing is not
        # a place the next one should open at.
        with guard:
            want.pop("stop", None)
            want.pop("seek", None)
        step, job["step"] = job["step"], 1
        job["paused"] = False   # a skip while paused lands playing
        advance(job, step)
    finish()                    # queue finished: nothing is playing any more

def start_playing(job):
    """Put a queue on the player. True once something is actually playing.

    This is the only way playback ever starts, and it asks before it acts: a
    player that is already running is handed the new queue and swaps to it,
    keeping the one mpv it has always had. A second player is started only
    when nobody answers - and even then that process takes the lock before it
    makes a sound, so the case this all exists for (two windows, or a window
    and a terminal, asking at the same moment) ends with one player playing
    what was asked for last, rather than two playing at once."""
    if send("play", job=job):
        return True
    # Nothing playing, so start something. The queue goes in a file, not on
    # the command line: Linux caps a single argument at 128 KB, and a library
    # is its own weight in names and paths - so passing it as an argument made
    # every library past a few hundred songs fail to play at all. The file is
    # named after this process so that two of us racing cannot read, or
    # delete, each other's.
    handover = "%s.%d" % (JOB, os.getpid())
    save_json(handover, job)
    subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__), "_player", handover],
                     start_new_session=True, stdin=NULL, stdout=NULL, stderr=NULL)
    for _ in range(60):         # up to 3s for it to be answering for itself
        if playing(0.2):
            return True
        time.sleep(0.05)
    return False

def landed(st, timeout=1.0):
    """What is playing once a skip or back has taken effect. Under shuffle the
    CLI can't work this out in advance - only the player picks - so read it
    back instead of guessing. `seq` rather than the song name, so a `back` that
    restarts the current song still registers."""
    seq, end = st.get("seq", 0), time.time() + timeout
    while time.time() < end:
        time.sleep(0.05)
        new = playing()
        if not new:
            return None                     # queue ran out
        if new.get("seq", 0) != seq:
            return new.get("song")
    return playing().get("song")

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- youtube
# Searching and downloading both go through yt-dlp, imported rather than run as
# a program: importing it costs about a second, starting the executable costs
# several.
#
# It is commonly on one machine more than once - the copy shipped beside this
# app, a current zipapp in ~/.local/bin, a stale one from the distro - and
# YouTube changes how it hands out audio often enough that only the newest
# extractor still reaches any: an old one can search, and then finds nothing
# but storyboard thumbnails where the audio should be. So every copy is looked
# at and the newest is the one imported. Versions are dates, `2026.08.19`, so
# they compare as plain strings.

YTDLP_MIN = "2025"          # older extractors can still search, but not download
CACHE_WAIT = 600            # seconds `download` will wait on a cache already
                            # filling before giving up and fetching it itself
CACHE_GRACE = 5             # ...and how long `stream --to` gives a fetch it
                            # started in the same breath to claim the song,
                            # before an empty cache means nobody ever will
RESOLVE_TTL = 3600          # how long a looked-up stream URL is trusted for.
                            # YouTube's own last about six hours; an hour is
                            # short enough that a stale one is never played
YTDLP_GONE = "yt-dlp isn't installed - run `simplmusik update`"
SEARCH_N = 8                # results a search comes back with
SEARCH_MAX = 40             # ...and the most it will, however many you ask for
SHOWN_TTL = 3600            # how long a printed list's numbering is worth
                            # taking - a working session, and no longer
AMBIGUOUS = 4               # how many near-misses a "which one?" names

def zipapp_version(path):
    """The version of a yt-dlp zipapp, read without importing it, or None if
    `path` isn't one."""
    try:
        with zipfile.ZipFile(path) as z:
            for line in z.read("yt_dlp/version.py").decode().splitlines():
                if line.startswith("__version__"):
                    return line.split("=", 1)[1].strip().strip("'\"")
    except (OSError, KeyError, ValueError, zipfile.BadZipFile):
        pass
    return None

_ytdlp = []                 # one-slot cache: [] not looked yet, [None] not there

def ytdlp():
    """The newest yt-dlp on this machine, imported, or None if there is none.

    Four places one can be: installed as a package, on the PATH, fetched by
    `update` into your own folder, or shipped beside the app. The last two are
    what let this work with nothing installed at all - and they are separate
    because the shipped copy may belong to root, which `update` cannot write."""
    if _ytdlp:
        return _ytdlp[0]
    try:
        import yt_dlp as mod
    except ImportError:
        mod = None
    best = None
    newest = mod.version.__version__ if mod else None
    for path in (shutil.which("yt-dlp"), YTDLP_USER, YTDLP_BUNDLED):
        zv = zipapp_version(path) if path else None
        if zv and (newest is None or zv > newest):
            best, newest = path, zv
    if best:
        # A zipapp is a zip of the package itself, so the path to it is a path
        # the import machinery can read a package out of.
        sys.path.insert(0, best)
        for name in [n for n in sys.modules if n.split(".")[0] == "yt_dlp"]:
            del sys.modules[name]
        try:
            import yt_dlp as mod
        except ImportError:
            pass
    _ytdlp.append(mod)
    return mod

def video_id(s):
    """A bare video id, from an id or from any shape of YouTube link."""
    s = (s or "").strip()
    if re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9_-]{11}", s):
        return s
    m = re.search(r"(?:v=|/shorts/|/embed/|/live/|youtu\.be/)([A-Za-z0-9_-]{11})", s)
    return m.group(1) if m else None

# ------------------------------------------------------------- naming a song
# There are three ways to say which song you mean, and every command that
# takes one takes all three: the number printed beside it in the last list you
# were shown, its filename, or enough of its filename to tell it from every
# other song.
#
# The number is the reason any of this exists. `search`, `songs` and `list`
# all print numbered lists, and the numbers are only worth printing if the
# next command will take one - otherwise the list is something to copy a
# filename out of, and the filenames here read `Artist - Title (Official
# Video).mp3` with the odd fullwidth quote in them, which is not something
# anybody types twice.
#
# So a listing writes down what it showed. It is the one piece of state a
# command leaves behind for the next one, and it expires, which is the point:
# a number is shorthand for "the third one you just showed me", and that stops
# being true long before a file on disk does. An hour is a working session.
# After it a number is only a number again, and a song actually called 3 wins.
#
# One list at a time, because there is one you were last shown. A search in
# the window writes here too - it is the same person asking - and the newest
# listing is the one the numbers belong to, wherever it was printed.

def remember_shown(kind, what, results):
    """Write down a numbered list, so its numbering means something to the
    command after it. Never fatal: a list that couldn't be written down is
    still a list you can read, and naming a song still works."""
    try:
        save_json(SHOWN, {"at": time.time(), "kind": kind,
                          "what": what, "results": results})
    except OSError:
        pass

def last_shown():
    """The last numbered list, if it is recent enough to still mean anything."""
    got = load_json(SHOWN, None)
    if not isinstance(got, dict) or not got.get("results"):
        return None
    return None if time.time() - (got.get("at") or 0) > SHOWN_TTL else got

def stale_number(ref):
    """The hint a bare number earns when the list it meant has expired, or ""
    when it has earned none.

    Only ever added to an answer that was going to be a no anyway: a number is
    a perfectly good word, and a song actually called 3 must still be findable
    by name an hour after the last search. So this explains a failure rather
    than causing one."""
    if not re.fullmatch(r"\s*\d+\s*", ref or "") or last_shown():
        return ""
    return (" - and the last list was over an hour ago, so %s is a word now, "
            "not a number: search or list again" % ref.strip()
            if isinstance(load_json(SHOWN, None), dict) else "")

def hunt(query, names):
    """Songs whose filename carries every word of the query, in any order and
    in any case.

    Every word rather than the whole phrase, because a filename reads
    `Artist - Title (Official Video).mp3` and nobody types the middle of it:
    matching the words separately is what lets `wham go go` find the song that
    has `wham` and `go` in it with a title's worth of punctuation in between.

    The order is the order it was given, which is the library's own - newest
    first - so the newest match comes first, here as everywhere else."""
    terms = (query or "").lower().split()
    return [n for n in names if all(t in n.lower() for t in terms)] if terms else []

def numbered(ref):
    """What a bare number names, as (row, why not).

    Both None when it isn't a number at all, and when there is no listing
    recent enough for it to be one - in that case it is simply a word, and
    whatever is actually called that gets to win."""
    if not re.fullmatch(r"\s*\d+\s*", ref or ""):
        return None, None
    got = last_shown()
    if not got:
        return None, None
    results, i = got["results"], int(ref)
    if 1 <= i <= len(results):
        return dict(results[i - 1], kind=got.get("kind")), None
    return None, ("there is no %d in %s - it had %d"
                  % (i, got.get("what") or "the last list", len(results)))

def pick(ref, idx, files=False):
    """What one word on the command line names, as (kind, value, why not):

        ("song", filename, None)      a song, by number or by name
        ("youtube", row, None)        a result of the last YouTube search
        (None, None, "...")           it named something, and named it wrong
        (None, None, None)            nothing here knows it - a path, maybe

    Tried in that order on purpose. The number goes first because it is the
    most recent thing you were shown. The whole filename goes before a part of
    one, so that a song can always be named outright however many other songs
    contain its name. And `files` stops the guessing where a real file is a
    real possibility: `add` may be being handed something to import, and a file
    that exists is never overruled by a guess at what you meant.

    A number names the row and nothing more - whether the song behind it is
    still in your music folder is the caller's question, because the answer
    differs: it is what stops `play`, and it is exactly what `remove` is for."""
    ref = (ref or "").strip()
    if not ref:
        return None, None, "which song?"
    row, why = numbered(ref)
    if why:
        return None, None, why
    if row:
        if row.get("kind") == "youtube":
            return "youtube", row, None
        return "song", row.get("file"), None
    name = os.path.basename(ref)
    if name in idx:
        return "song", name, None
    if files and os.path.exists(ref):
        return None, None, None         # a file to import: not ours to guess at
    hits = hunt(ref, list(idx))
    if len(hits) == 1:
        return "song", hits[0], None
    if hits:
        # Cut short, and each one cut short: these are full filenames, and
        # four of them at their real length is a paragraph to read for a
        # question that only wants another word or two of the name.
        return None, None, ("'%s' matches %d songs: %s%s - say which, or "
                            "`search %s` to see them"
                            % (ref, len(hits),
                               ", ".join(h[:40].rstrip() + ("..." if len(h) > 40 else "")
                                         for h in hits[:AMBIGUOUS]),
                               ", ..." if len(hits) > AMBIGUOUS else "", ref)
                            ) + stale_number(ref)
    return None, None, None

def song_ref(ref, idx):
    """A song, numbered or named, as (filename, why not). The name only: a
    song a playlist still points at after the file has gone is named the same
    way as any other, and dropping it is the whole of what `remove` does."""
    kind, val, why = pick(ref, idx)
    if kind == "song":
        return val, None
    if kind == "youtube":
        return None, ("result %s is on YouTube, not in your library - "
                      "`stream %s` plays it, `download %s` fetches it"
                      % (ref, ref, ref))
    return None, why

def video_ref(ref):
    """A video id, from an id, a link, or the number of a YouTube search
    result, as (id, why not)."""
    row, why = numbered(ref)
    if why:
        return None, why
    if row:
        if row.get("kind") != "youtube":
            return None, ("result %s is %s, which is already in your library "
                          "- `play %s` plays it" % (ref, row.get("file"), ref))
        return row.get("id"), None
    vid = video_id(ref)
    return (vid, None) if vid else (
        None, "not a YouTube video id, link or search result: %s" % ref)

def numbers(kind, what, rows):
    """Number a list of songs on its way to being printed, and remember the
    numbering. One place does this so that every list you can be shown is
    numbered the same way and means the same thing afterwards."""
    remember_shown(kind, what, [{"n": i + 1, "file": r} for i, r in enumerate(rows)])
    return ["%2d. %s" % (i + 1, r) for i, r in enumerate(rows)]

def join(stem, name, path):
    """Put one library song on the top of a playlist, and say whether that
    was anything new. A playlist holds a song once: where it sits is when it
    was added, so adding it again would only move it."""
    songs = songs_of(stem)
    if any(p["file"] == name for p in songs):
        return False
    m = meta(stem)
    m["songs"] = store([{"file": name, "path": path}] + songs)
    write_meta(stem, m)
    return True

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ commands

def cmd_status(args):
    lib, pls, st, dir_ = library(), playlists(), playing(), folder()
    # Where we are in the playlist is worked out by the player, which is the
    # one that knows: shuffled, there is no last track, so only count played.
    track = st.get("track")
    info = {"folder": dir_, "songs": len(lib), "playlists": len(pls),
            "playing": bool(st), "paused": bool(st.get("paused")),
            "current_playlist": st.get("playlist"), "current_song": st.get("song"),
            "track": track, "played": st.get("index", 0) + 1 if st else None,
            "queue": st.get("queue") or None,
            "shuffle": shuffling(),
            "playlists_list": [p["name"] for p in pls]}
    if not st:
        now = "playing: nothing"
    else:
        pos = ("#%d, shuffled" % (st["index"] + 1) if shuffling()
               else "%d/%d" % (track, len(st["songs"])) if track else "")
        now = "%s %s  (%s%s)" % ("paused:" if st.get("paused") else "playing:",
                                 st["song"], st.get("playlist") or "library",
                                 ", " + pos if pos else "")
    emit(args.json, info, "folder:      %s\nsongs:       %d\nplaylists:   %d\nnow %s"
         % (dir_, len(lib), len(pls), now))
    return 0

# What `stats` will answer, and what each one prints. The bare command prints
# the summary and the head of the two tables under it, because that is the
# question almost everybody is asking; a topic is for when you want the whole
# of one of them.
TOPICS = ("summary", "songs", "playlists", "skips", "time", "log")

def when(at, clock=False):
    """A logged moment, printed. Dates rather than 'three days ago': the log
    goes back years and a relative date stops meaning anything past a week."""
    if not at:
        return "never"
    return time.strftime("%-d %b %Y" + (" %H:%M" if clock else ""),
                         time.localtime(at))

def clip(s, n):
    """A song name cut to fit a column. The whole of it is always in --json,
    so nothing is lost - only the table is kept to a width you can read."""
    s = s or ""
    return s if len(s) <= n else s[:n - 1] + "…"

def since_from(s):
    """How far back to read: `7d`, `24h`, `2w`, or `all`. False if it is none
    of those, which the caller turns into an error - a window we couldn't read
    should not quietly become the whole history."""
    s = (s or "").strip().lower()
    if not s or s == "all":
        return None
    mul = {"h": 3600.0, "d": 86400.0, "w": 604800.0}.get(s[-1])
    try:
        n = float(s[:-1] if mul else s)
    except ValueError:
        return False
    return time.time() - n * (mul or 86400.0)

def table(groups, count, head="song"):
    """One ranked table. Time first and the count beside it, always: the two
    columns disagree often enough that showing either one alone is a claim we
    can't stand behind."""
    lines = ["  %-9s %5s %5s %5s  %s"
             % ("LISTENED", "PLAYS", "DONE", "SKIP", head.upper())]
    for g in groups[:count]:
        lines.append("  %-9s %5d %5d %5d  %s"
                     % (spell_secs(g["heard"]), g["plays"], g["finished"],
                        g["active"], clip(g["name"], 46)))
    return "\n".join(lines)

def summary_text(s, count):
    """The whole picture in a dozen lines."""
    out = ["listening    %s across %d songs, %d played out"
           % (spell_secs(s["heard"]), s["songs"], s["finished"])]
    if s["finish_rate"] is not None:
        out.append("finished     %d%%  - %d of the %d spans you had a view on"
                   % (round(s["finish_rate"]), s["finished"], s["judged"]))
        out.append("cut short    %d  - %d skipped, %d swapped away, %d went back"
                   % (s["active"], s["skipped"], s["swapped"], s["backed"]))
    if s["bail_median_secs"] is not None:
        out.append("you leave    %s in, typically  - %s"
                   % (spell_secs(s["bail_median_secs"]),
                      ", ".join("%d %s" % (n, b) for b, n in s["bail"].items() if n)))
    out.append("chosen       %d you picked, %d the shuffle picked"
               % (s["chosen"], s["drawn"]))
    if s["sources"]:
        out.append("sources      " + ", ".join("%d %s" % (n, k)
                                               for k, n in s["sources"].items()))
    out.append("evenings     %d, the longest %s of music"
               % (s["sessions"], spell_secs(s["longest_session"])))
    out.append("history      %s to %s" % (when(s["first"]), when(s["last"])))
    songs = rank(s["by_song"], "time", count)
    pools = rank(s["by_playlist"], "time", count)
    out.append("\nmost listened to\n" + table(songs, count))
    if len(pools) > 1 or (pools and pools[0]["name"] != LIBRARY_NAME):
        out.append("\nlists\n" + table(pools, count, "list"))
    out.append("\n`simplmusik stats %s` for more."
               % "`, `simplmusik stats ".join(t for t in TOPICS if t != "summary"))
    return "\n".join(out)

def skips_text(s, count):
    """Where you leave songs, and which ones you leave. Two questions that
    look like one until you see that most bails are in the first five seconds
    and a few are two thirds of the way in - which are not the same feeling."""
    out = ["cut short    %d of %d spans you had a view on" % (s["active"], s["judged"])]
    if s["bail_median_secs"] is not None:
        out.append("typically    %s in, %g%% of the way through"
                   % (spell_secs(s["bail_median_secs"]), s["bail_median_frac"] or 0))
    out.append("")
    worst = max([n for n in s["bail"].values()] or [0]) or 1
    for b in BAILS:
        n = s["bail"][b]
        if n or b != "unknown":
            out.append("  %-13s %-24s %3d" % (b, "█" * int(round(20.0 * n / worst)), n))
    skipped = [g for g in rank(s["by_song"], "skips", None) if g["active"]]
    if skipped:
        out.append("\nmost cut short\n" + table(skipped, count))
    return "\n".join(out)

def time_text(s, rows, count):
    """When you listen. The clock is the shape of your day; the evenings are
    the shape of your week."""
    peak = max(s["clock"].values() or [0]) or 1
    out = ["by hour of day"]
    # Only the hours you have ever listened in. An empty hour is a row
    # saying nothing, and a fresh log would be twenty-three of those
    # around the one line that means something; a year of listening
    # fills nearly all of them in, so the shape of a day still draws.
    for h in sorted(k for k, n in s["clock"].items() if n):
        n = s["clock"][h]
        out.append("  %s:00  %-30s %3d"
                   % (h, "█" * int(round(30.0 * n / peak)), n))
    every = sessions(rows)[-count:]
    if every:
        out.append("\nlatest evenings")
        out.append("  %-17s %-9s %-9s %s" % ("STARTED", "LISTENED", "LASTED", "SONGS"))
        for e in reversed(every):
            out.append("  %-17s %-9s %-9s %d"
                       % (when(e["at"], clock=True), spell_secs(e["heard"]),
                          spell_secs(e["length"]), e["plays"]))
    return "\n".join(out)

def log_text(rows, count):
    """The spans themselves, newest first - what a stats page is built out of,
    for when a number on one looks wrong and you want to see why."""
    out = ["  %-17s %-6s %-6s %-8s %s"
           % ("WHEN", "PICK", "END", "LISTENED", "SONG")]
    for r in reversed(rows[-count:]):
        out.append("  %-17s %-6s %-6s %-8s %s"
                   % (when(r["at"], clock=True), r.get("pick") or "-", r["end"],
                      spell_secs(r["heard"]), clip(r["song"], 40)))
    return "\n".join(out)

def cmd_stats(args):
    """What you have actually listened to, out of the log every played song
    writes a line to.

    Everything here is worked out from that file each time it is asked, which
    is the whole design: there is no tally kept anywhere to fall out of step,
    and deleting the log is the only thing that ever needs to mean anything.

    Time listened leads every table, and the play count sits beside it rather
    than instead of it. They disagree more often than you would think - four
    aborted starts hunting for the right version of a song will out-count the
    evening you spent inside one - and the count on its own is the number that
    would be wrong."""
    topic = (args.topic or "summary").lower()
    if topic not in TOPICS:
        return fail(args.json, "no such topic '%s' - try %s"
                    % (args.topic, ", ".join(TOPICS)), 2)
    since = since_from(args.since)
    if since is False:
        return fail(args.json, "can't read '%s' as a length of time - try 7d, "
                    "24h, 2w or all" % args.since, 2)
    count = args.count if args.count and args.count > 0 else (5 if topic == "summary" else 15)
    rows = read_plays(since=since)
    if not rows:
        return fail(args.json, "nothing played yet%s - the log fills up as you "
                    "listen, and lives at %s"
                    % (" in that window" if since else "", plays_path()), 2)
    s = stats(rows)
    by = (args.by or "time").lower()
    if by not in ORDERS:
        return fail(args.json, "can't sort by '%s' - try %s"
                    % (args.by, ", ".join(ORDERS)), 2)

    if topic == "songs":
        out = rank(s["by_song"], by, count)
        text = table(out, count)
    elif topic == "playlists":
        out = rank(s["by_playlist"], by, count)
        text = table(out, count, "list")
    elif topic == "skips":
        out, text = s, skips_text(s, count)
    elif topic == "time":
        out, text = s, time_text(s, rows, count)
    elif topic == "log":
        out, text = rows[-count:], log_text(rows, count)
    else:
        out, text = s, summary_text(s, count)
    return emit(args.json, out, text) or 0

def cmd_folder(args):
    """Your music folder, and choosing a different one. Everything under it
    comes with it, so there is never a subfolder to name as well; and there is
    only ever one, so choosing is all there is to do - nothing is ever taken
    away and left with none. Nothing here ever touches a file."""
    dir_, action = folder(), (args.action or "").lower()
    if not action:
        return emit(args.json, dir_, dir_) or 0
    if action != "set":
        return fail(args.json, "say `folder` or `folder set PATH`", 1)
    if args.path:
        path = os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(args.path))
    else:
        picked = choose_folder()
        if picked is None:
            return fail(args.json, "no folder picker on this machine - name the "
                        "folder instead: `simplmusik folder set PATH`", 1)
        if not picked:
            # Closing the dialog isn't an error, it's an answer: nothing here
            # changed, and there is nothing to complain about.
            return emit(args.json, {"folder": dir_, "changed": False},
                        "nothing picked") or 0
        path = os.path.abspath(picked)
    if not os.path.isdir(path):
        return fail(args.json, "no such folder: %s" % path, 2)
    if path == dir_:
        # Choosing the folder you are already on is not a mistake to report,
        # it is simply nothing to do.
        return emit(args.json, {"folder": dir_, "changed": False},
                    "already your music folder: " + path) or 0
    was, log = playlists_dir(), plays_path()   # before the change, so the
    set_folder(path)                          # move has somewhere to follow
    rehome(was, log)            # both live in the folder, so both go with it
    return emit(args.json, {"folder": path, "changed": True},
                "music folder is now " + path) or 0

def cmd_levelling(args):
    """Levelling on, off, or as it stands. Changing it mid-song swaps the filter
    under the song that is playing, so the difference is heard at once, in the
    middle of a bar, with nothing restarted. Songs are measured as they are
    played, so turning it on doesn't stop to work through your library
    first."""
    action = (args.action or "").lower()
    if action and action not in ("on", "off"):
        return fail(args.json, "say `levelling`, `levelling on` or "
                    "`levelling off`", 1)
    on = levelling()
    if action:
        on = action == "on"
        set_setting("levelling", on)
        send("levelling")       # take effect in this song, not the next
    return emit(args.json, {"levelling": on},
                "levelling " + ("on" if on else "off")) or 0

def cmd_target(args):
    """Where levelling aims, or a new place to aim it. Like the switch itself,
    moving it swaps the filter under the song that is playing, so the whole
    library shifts under you as you move it and nothing is restarted. Nothing
    is re-measured either - the mean and the peak in the cache are the same two
    numbers whatever we aim at."""
    if args.db is None:
        return emit(args.json, {"target": target()},
                    "target %g dB" % target()) or 0
    try:
        want = round(float(args.db), 1)
    except ValueError:
        return fail(args.json, "give a target from %g to %g dB"
                    % (TARGET_LO, TARGET_HI), 1)
    if not TARGET_LO <= want <= TARGET_HI:
        return fail(args.json,
                    "the target goes from %g to %g dB - full scale is 0, so it "
                    "is a number below it, and louder than %g leaves too many "
                    "songs short of it to be level at all"
                    % (TARGET_LO, TARGET_HI, TARGET_HI), 1)
    set_setting("target", want)
    send("levelling")           # re-level where the song stands, as the switch does
    return emit(args.json, {"target": want}, "target %g dB" % want) or 0

def cmd_volume(args):
    """Volume, or a new one. Changing it takes effect where the song is: mpv
    is holding it, so there is nothing to restart and nothing to hear but the
    volume changing."""
    if args.level is None:
        return emit(args.json, {"volume": volume()}, "volume %d" % volume()) or 0
    try:
        want = int(args.level)
    except ValueError:
        return fail(args.json, "give a volume from 0 to 100", 1)
    if not 0 <= want <= 100:
        return fail(args.json, "volume goes from 0 to 100 - 100 is a song at "
                    "the loudness levelling set it to, and there is no louder", 1)
    set_setting("volume", want)
    send("volume")              # nothing playing is nothing to tell
    return emit(args.json, {"volume": want}, "volume %d" % want) or 0

def cmd_songs(args):
    lib = library()
    if not lib:
        return fail(args.json, "no songs in %s - copy some music in, or choose "
                    "a different folder with `simplmusik folder set PATH`"
                    % folder(), 2)
    return emit(args.json, lib,
                "\n".join(numbers("library", LIBRARY_NAME, lib))) or 0

def cmd_list(args):
    """Playlists, or the songs in one - and the library is one of them.

    It has no file and never will: it is every song in your music folder,
    which is not a list anybody made. But it is what the window shows at the
    top of its sidebar and what `play` starts by default, so it is named here
    rather than being the one list you have to know a separate command for.
    `songs` is still that command, and `list "All songs"` is the same thing
    said the way you would say it of any other list."""
    if not args.playlist:
        rows = [{"name": LIBRARY_NAME, "songs": len(library()), "library": True}]
        rows += [{"name": p["name"], "songs": len(p["songs"]), "library": False}
                 for p in playlists()]
        return emit(args.json, rows,
                    "\n".join("%-34s %3d" % (r["name"] + (" (%s)" % LIBRARY_NOTE
                                                          if r["library"] else ""),
                                             r["songs"]) for r in rows)) or 0
    stem = find(args.playlist)
    if not stem:
        # The library, asked for by name. `songs` prints exactly this, so it
        # prints it: two commands, one answer, and no second opinion about the
        # order or about what an empty music folder should say.
        if is_library(args.playlist):
            return cmd_songs(args)
        return fail(args.json, "no playlist '%s'" % args.playlist, 2)
    songs = songs_of(stem)
    if not songs:
        return fail(args.json, "playlist '%s' is empty" % args.playlist, 2)
    lines = numbers("library", "playlist '%s'" % stem, [p["file"] for p in songs])
    return emit(args.json, [{"n": i + 1, "file": p["file"], "found": bool(p["path"])}
                            for i, p in enumerate(songs)],
                "\n".join(line + ("" if p["path"] else "   (not found)")
                          for line, p in zip(lines, songs))) or 0

def cmd_create(args):
    stem = os.path.basename(args.name.rstrip("/"))
    if not stem:
        return fail(args.json, "give the playlist a name", 1)
    if find(stem):
        return fail(args.json, "playlist already exists: %s" % stem, 1)
    write_meta(stem, {"name": args.name, "created": round(time.time(), 3),
                      "order": "newest", "songs": []})
    if args.cover:
        try:
            set_cover(stem, args.cover)
        except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
            # The playlist is made either way: a picture that wouldn't copy is
            # no reason to throw away the name you just gave it.
            return fail(args.json, "created %s, but its cover art didn't: %s"
                        % (stem, e), 1)
    return emit(args.json, {"created": stem, "path": playlist_file(stem),
                            "cover": cover_path(stem)},
                "created playlist: " + stem) or 0

def cmd_rename(args):
    """Rename a playlist. Only the name you see changes: the file keeps the
    stem it was made with, which is what the player, the state file and the
    window all name a playlist by - so renaming one that is playing can't
    unhitch it from what is sounding."""
    stem = find(args.playlist)
    if not stem:
        return fail(args.json, "no playlist '%s'" % args.playlist, 2)
    name = (args.name or "").strip()
    if not name:
        return fail(args.json, "give the playlist a name", 1)
    if "/" in name:
        return fail(args.json, "a playlist name can't have a '/' in it", 1)
    other = find(name)
    if other and other != stem:
        return fail(args.json, "another playlist is already called '%s'" % name, 1)
    was = meta(stem).get("name") or stem
    m = meta(stem)
    m["name"] = name
    write_meta(stem, m)
    return emit(args.json, {"playlist": stem, "was": was, "name": name},
                "renamed %s to %s" % (was, name)) or 0

def cmd_cover(args):
    """A playlist's cover art: shown, set from an image, or dropped."""
    stem = find(args.playlist)
    if not stem:
        return fail(args.json, "no playlist '%s'" % args.playlist, 2)
    if args.clear:
        had = drop_cover(stem)
        return emit(args.json, {"playlist": stem, "cover": None},
                    "cover art removed" if had else "it hadn't any cover art") or 0
    if not args.image:
        path = cover_path(stem)
        if not path:
            return fail(args.json, "playlist '%s' has no cover art" % stem, 2)
        return emit(args.json, {"playlist": stem, "cover": path}, path) or 0
    try:
        path = set_cover(stem, args.image)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as e:
        return fail(args.json, str(e), 1)
    return emit(args.json, {"playlist": stem, "cover": path},
                "cover art set: " + path) or 0

def cmd_add(args):
    """Add songs to a playlist, or to the library itself.

    Three ways to name each one, and they all end in the same place: the
    number a search printed beside it, a song already in your library - whole
    name or enough of it - or a path to a file to import. A YouTube result is
    fetched first and then added, so `search --yt` and then `add` is the whole
    of what the window's download button does from inside a playlist.

    `add "All songs" ...` imports into the library and puts the song in no
    playlist, which is what the library being a list you can add to means."""
    stem = find(args.playlist)
    to_library = stem is None and is_library(args.playlist)
    if stem is None and not to_library:
        return fail(args.json, "no playlist '%s'" % args.playlist, 2)
    dest, idx = folder(), index()
    songs = [] if to_library else songs_of(stem)
    # What counts as already there: the playlist's own songs, or - adding to
    # the library - every song in it, since that is the same question.
    have = set(idx) if to_library else {p["file"] for p in songs}
    added, failed, fresh = [], [], []
    for src in args.files:
        kind, val, why = pick(src, idx, files=True)
        if why:
            failed.append(why)
            continue
        if kind == "youtube":
            got, why = fetch_id(val["id"])
            if not got:
                failed.append("%s (%s)" % (val.get("title") or val["id"], why))
                continue
            idx = index()               # it is a library song now
            name, path = got["song"], idx.get(got["song"])
        elif kind == "song":
            # A numbered row whose file has since gone is still that row, and
            # a playlist can hold a song it can't find - it says so and plays
            # past it. So `path` is allowed to be None here.
            name, path = val, idx.get(val)
            if path and os.path.isfile(src) and not os.path.samefile(src, path):
                # One flat namespace: importing over a different song of the
                # same name would lose it silently, so refuse instead.
                failed.append("%s (a different song already has that name)" % name)
                continue
        elif not os.path.exists(src):
            # Not a number, not a song in your library, and no file there
            # either - so there is nothing this could have meant.
            failed.append("nothing in your library matches '%s', and there's "
                          "no file there to import" % src + stale_number(src))
            continue
        else:
            name = os.path.basename(src)
            try:
                os.makedirs(dest, exist_ok=True)
                path = os.path.join(dest, name)
                # copy, not copy2: an imported song dates from the import,
                # not from whenever the file you imported was last written -
                # a song you just added reads as one you just added, and the
                # library's order is the order things turned up in it.
                shutil.copy(src, path)
                idx[name] = path
            except OSError as e:
                failed.append("%s (%s)" % (name, e))
                continue
        if name not in have:
            have.add(name)
            fresh.append({"file": name, "path": path})
            added.append(name)
    # On the top of the list, not the bottom: a playlist reads newest first,
    # and where a song sits in the file is the only record of when it was
    # added. Several at once keep the order you named them in, which is the
    # only thing to go on when they arrive in the same breath. Adding to the
    # library is only the import - there is no list to write.
    if not to_library and fresh:
        m = meta(stem)
        m["songs"] = store(fresh + songs)
        write_meta(stem, m)
    where = LIBRARY_NAME if to_library else stem
    text = "\n".join(t for t in ("added %d song(s) to %s" % (len(added), where) if added else "",
                                 "couldn't add: " + ", ".join(failed) if failed else "") if t)
    emit(args.json, {"added": added, "failed": failed, "playlist": where},
         text or "nothing to add")
    return 0 if added else 1

def cmd_remove(args):
    """Two removes, told apart by how many names you give. `remove PLAYLIST
    SONG` only edits that list. `remove SONG` is the library one: it deletes
    the file, and every list pointing at it goes with it. A song that is only
    a not-found row - the file is already gone - has nothing to delete, so
    that one just clears the lists still naming it.

    Naming the library is the same as naming nothing, so `remove "All songs"
    SONG` is the library one said the long way round. Either way the song
    itself can be a number from the last list you were shown, which is what
    makes `list` and then `remove` two steps rather than two steps and a
    filename to copy."""
    idx = index()
    stem = find(args.playlist) if args.song is not None else None
    if args.song is not None and not stem and not is_library(args.playlist):
        return fail(args.json, "no playlist '%s'" % args.playlist, 2)

    if stem is None:
        ref = args.song if args.song is not None else args.playlist
        got, why = song_ref(ref, idx)
        song = got or os.path.basename(ref or "")
        path = idx.get(song)
        holders = [p["file"] for p in playlists()
                   if any(x["file"] == song for x in p["songs"])]
        if not path and not holders:
            return fail(args.json, why or
                        ("no song '%s' in your library (to drop one from "
                         "a playlist: remove PLAYLIST SONG)" % song), 2)
        if path:
            try:
                os.remove(path)         # the file first: if this fails,
            except OSError as e:        # nothing has been changed yet
                return fail(args.json, "couldn't delete %s (%s)" % (song, e), 1)
        for holder in holders:
            m = meta(holder)
            m["songs"] = [e for e in (m.get("songs") or []) if entry(e)[0] != song]
            write_meta(holder, m)
        # A song being played as it is deleted stops. mpv holds the file
        # open and would happily sound a deleted song to the end, which is the
        # one thing that would make the library and what you hear disagree.
        # Nothing to move on to either, so the honest answer is nothing playing.
        st = playing()
        stopped = bool(st and st.get("song") == song)
        if stopped:
            stop_player()
        return emit(args.json, {"deleted": song, "playlists": holders,
                                "file": bool(path), "stopped": stopped},
                    "%s %s%s%s" % ("deleted" if path else "dropped missing", song,
                                   " (was in %s)" % ", ".join(holders)
                                   if holders else "",
                                   " - playback stopped" if stopped else "")) or 0

    songs = songs_of(stem)
    got, _ = song_ref(args.song, idx)
    song = got or os.path.basename(args.song)
    if not any(p["file"] == song for p in songs):
        return fail(args.json, "no song '%s' in '%s'" % (song, stem), 2)
    m = meta(stem)
    m["songs"] = store([p for p in songs if p["file"] != song])
    write_meta(stem, m)
    return emit(args.json, {"removed": song, "playlist": stem},
                "removed %s from %s (the file stays in your library)" % (song, stem)) or 0

def cmd_delete(args):
    """Deleting a playlist deletes only the list. Every song in it lives in the
    library, which isn't touched - so there is nothing here worth confirming."""
    stem = find(args.playlist)
    if not stem:
        return fail(args.json, "no playlist '%s'" % args.playlist, 2)
    n = len(songs_of(stem))
    drop_cover(stem)            # its picture is ours, and nothing else's now
    os.remove(playlist_file(stem))
    return emit(args.json, {"deleted": stem, "songs_kept": n},
                "deleted playlist %s (%d song%s still in your library)"
                % (stem, n, "" if n == 1 else "s")) or 0

def cmd_play(args):
    st, idx = playing(), index()
    # `--shuffle` throws the one shuffle switch rather than shuffling this
    # playback only, so it is the same switch the `shuffle` command and the
    # button in the window throw - and it stays thrown afterwards.
    if getattr(args, "shuffle", False):
        set_setting("shuffle", True)
    shuffle = shuffling()
    name, song = args.playlist, args.song
    # `--library` asks for the library by name, which nothing else can: a bare
    # `play` carries on with the playlist that is going, and naming a song to
    # mean the library gives away which song it opens on. It is how the window
    # starts the library shuffled and lets the draw choose, rather than picking
    # a song itself and calling it a start.
    library = getattr(args, "library", False)
    # ...and `All songs` asks for it the way you would ask for any other list,
    # which is what having a name for it is for. A playlist actually called
    # that wins: `find` is asked first.
    if name and not find(name) and is_library(name):
        library, name = True, None
    # A song named where a playlist could go means the library, starting there
    # - and it means the library even while a playlist is playing, so
    # `play SONG` never quietly scopes itself to whatever happened to be on.
    # Numbered or named: it is the same lookup `add` does, so the song a
    # search just numbered is the song that plays.
    if song:
        song, why = song_ref(song, idx)
        if why:
            return fail(args.json, why, 2)
    elif name and not find(name):
        got, why = song_ref(name, idx)
        if got:
            name, song = None, got
        elif why:
            return fail(args.json, why, 2)
    if library:
        name, song = None, name or song
    elif not name:
        # Nothing named a list, so where a song plays is worked out from where
        # it actually is. A bare `play` carries on with the playlist that is
        # going. A song on its own plays inside that playlist if the playlist
        # has it - clicking a song you can see in the list you are in should
        # not quietly drop you out of it - and in the library if it hasn't,
        # because the song is real and being somewhere else is no reason to
        # refuse it. A playlist named outright is never overruled by either.
        on = find(st.get("playlist") or "")
        if not song:
            name = st.get("playlist")
        elif on:
            here = os.path.basename(song)
            name = on if any(p["file"] == here and p["path"]
                             for p in songs_of(on)) else None

    if name:
        stem = find(name)
        if not stem:
            return fail(args.json, "no playlist or song '%s'" % name
                        + stale_number(name), 2)
        name, listed = stem, songs_of(stem)
    else:
        listed = [{"file": n, "path": idx[n]} for n in idx]
    where = "playlist '%s'" % name if name else "your library"
    # Only songs we can actually find go in the queue; the not-found ones stay
    # in the playlist and are simply passed over, the way a gap in a shelf is.
    songs = [p["file"] for p in listed if p["path"]]
    paths = {p["file"]: p["path"] for p in listed if p["path"]}
    if not songs:
        return fail(args.json, "nothing in %s is in your music folder" % where
                    if listed else "%s is empty" % where, 2)

    # Shuffled with no song named, the opening song is a draw like every other
    # one - so a playlist doesn't always start the same, and so it is levelled
    # against what this playlist has already heard rather than landing
    # anywhere. The counts come from the player because that is where they
    # live, and the ones that matter are this playlist's own: nothing playing,
    # or a playlist not played yet, is an empty tally either way.
    session = ask({"op": "plays"}) or {}
    key = name or ""
    # What this playlist has heard lately, and then the song sounding right
    # now - which is the most recent thing heard by definition, even when it
    # belongs to some other playlist and the window is the only thing here
    # that will care about it.
    lately = [s for s in (session.get("heard") or {}).get(key) or []
              if s != st.get("song")] + ([st["song"]] if st.get("song") else [])
    drawn = shuffle and not song
    start = (draw(songs, (session.get("plays") or {}).get(key) or {}, lately)
             if drawn else songs[0])
    if song:
        start = os.path.basename(song)
        if start not in songs:
            return fail(args.json,
                        "'%s' isn't in your music folder" % start
                        if any(p["file"] == start for p in listed)
                        else "no song '%s' in %s" % (start, where), 2)
    if not shutil.which("mpv"):
        return fail(args.json, "mpv not found - install mpv", 1)

    # `songs` is the pool, `order` is only what has actually been played, so
    # naming a song starts there and carries on rather than stopping after it.
    # `paths` is where each one is: worked out once here, so the player never
    # has to search for a file and the queue can't shift under it mid-song.
    # `drawn` says whether the shuffle chose the opening song. Whether it
    # counts as a play is the player's to decide - it is the one that knows
    # whether this playlist is being started or moved about in.
    job = {"playlist": name, "songs": songs, "paths": paths, "order": [start],
           "index": 0, "song": start, "paused": False, "drawn": drawn,
           "shuffle": shuffle, "step": 1, "seq": 0}
    if not start_playing(job):
        return fail(args.json, "couldn't start the player", 1)
    # Only once it is actually sounding: a playlist that couldn't be started is
    # not one you played, and the sidebar shouldn't lift it as though it were.
    if name:
        mark_played(name)
    return emit(args.json, {"playing": name or "library", "song": start,
                            "songs": len(songs), "shuffle": shuffle},
                "playing %s: %s (%d song%s%s)"
                % (name or "your library", start, len(songs),
                   "" if len(songs) == 1 else "s", ", shuffled" if shuffle else "")) or 0

def cmd_shuffle(args):
    """Shuffle on, off, or flipped. It only ever moves the switch: nothing
    starts playing that wasn't, and anything already playing keeps the song it
    is on - only what comes after it changes. `play --shuffle` is how you turn
    it on and start something in the one breath."""
    action = (args.action or "").lower()
    if action and action not in ("on", "off"):
        return fail(args.json, "say `shuffle`, `shuffle on` or `shuffle off`", 1)
    on = action == "on" if action else not shuffling()
    set_setting("shuffle", on)
    send("shuffle", on=on)      # nothing playing is nothing to tell
    return emit(args.json, {"shuffle": on}, "shuffle " + ("on" if on else "off")) or 0

def cmd_pause(args, want=True):
    """Hold the song, or let it go again - and say what actually happened.

    One question to the player, which is the one thing that knows whether it
    was already paused, so what is printed is its answer rather than what it
    was asked for. Asking what was playing and then telling it to pause would
    be two answers from two moments, with the song free to change in between
    and nothing gained: the player has to make the decision either way."""
    r = ask({"op": "pause", "want": want})
    if not (r and r.get("ok")):
        return fail(args.json, "nothing playing", 1)
    paused, song = bool(r.get("paused")), r.get("song") or "the song"
    state = "paused" if paused else "playing"
    return emit(args.json,
                {"paused": paused, "song": r.get("song"),
                 "changed": bool(r.get("changed"))},
                "%s %s" % ("paused" if paused else "resumed", song)
                if r.get("changed") else "%s is already %s" % (song, state)) or 0

def cmd_move(args, op):
    st = playing()
    if not st:
        return fail(args.json, "nothing playing", 1)
    send(op)
    landed_on = landed(st)
    return emit(args.json, {"op": op, "was": st["song"], "now": landed_on},
                "%s: %s -> %s" % (op, st["song"], landed_on or "end of queue")) or 0

def cmd_seek(args):
    """Jump within the song that is playing. A relative jump is resolved here,
    against the state file, so the player is only ever handed one absolute
    position and needs to know nothing about how it was asked for.

    Seeking past the end isn't clamped: mpv run off the end of a song simply
    ends it, which moves to the next one - a fair reading of "seek to the
    end". Callers that do know the length (the UI does) clamp before
    asking."""
    st = playing()
    if not st:
        return fail(args.json, "nothing playing", 1)
    t = parse_time(args.time)
    if t is None:
        return fail(args.json, "give a position like 1:23, 83, +30 or -10", 1)
    secs, rel = t
    at = max(0.0, st.get("elapsed", 0.0) + secs if rel else secs)
    if not send("seek", pos=at):
        return fail(args.json, "nothing playing", 1)
    return emit(args.json, {"seek": round(at, 1), "song": st["song"]},
                "%s -> %s" % (st["song"], clock(at))) or 0

def cmd_stop(args):
    was = stop_player()
    return emit(args.json, {"stopped": was}, "stopped" if was else "nothing playing") or 0

def cmd_search(args):
    """Search your library, or YouTube with `--yt`.

    Your own songs by default because that is the common question - have I got
    this? - and it is answered off the disk in no time at all, where YouTube is
    a network round trip you asked for. The window has always shown the two
    halves in this order, its own songs above what YouTube has; this is the
    same order on one command with a switch.

    Either way the results come back numbered, and that numbering is what
    `add`, `play`, `download` and `stream` will take in place of a name."""
    query = " ".join(args.query).strip()
    if not query:
        return fail(args.json, "give me something to search for", 1)
    return (search_youtube if args.yt else search_library)(args, query)

def search_library(args, query):
    """Your own songs, newest first. There is no ceiling unless you ask for
    one: the whole thing is a walk you have already paid for, and `songs`
    prints the lot as it is - a search is that list with the misses taken out."""
    idx = index()
    hits = hunt(query, list(idx))
    if args.count:
        hits = hits[:max(1, int(args.count))]
    if not hits:
        return fail(args.json, "nothing in your library matches '%s' "
                    "(`search --yt %s` looks on YouTube)" % (query, query), 2)
    lines = numbers("library", "the search for '%s'" % query, hits)
    return emit(args.json, [{"n": i + 1, "file": n, "path": idx[n]}
                            for i, n in enumerate(hits)], "\n".join(lines)) or 0

def search_youtube(args, query):
    """Search YouTube for songs. `extract_flat` keeps this to the search page
    itself - no per-video page is ever fetched - which is the difference
    between about a second and about ten. `--count` asks for more than the
    usual handful, which is how the window's 'see more' gets a longer list."""
    m = ytdlp()
    if m is None:
        return fail(args.json, YTDLP_GONE, 1)
    n = max(1, min(int(args.count or SEARCH_N), SEARCH_MAX))
    try:
        with m.YoutubeDL({"quiet": True, "no_warnings": True, "extract_flat": True,
                          "skip_download": True, "ignoreerrors": True}) as y:
            found = y.extract_info("ytsearch%d:%s" % (n, query), download=False)
    except Exception as e:                  # yt-dlp raises a wide family of these
        return fail(args.json, "search failed: %s" % e, 1)
    out = []
    for e in (found or {}).get("entries") or []:
        if e and e.get("id"):
            out.append({"n": len(out) + 1, "id": e["id"],
                        "title": e.get("title") or e["id"],
                        "channel": e.get("channel") or e.get("uploader") or "",
                        "duration": e.get("duration")})
    if not out:
        return fail(args.json, "nothing found for '%s'" % query, 2)
    remember_shown("youtube", "the YouTube search for '%s'" % query,
                   [{"n": r["n"], "id": r["id"], "title": r["title"]} for r in out])
    text = "\n".join("%2d. %-52.52s %7s  %s" % (s["n"], s["title"],
                                                clock(s["duration"] or 0), s["id"])
                     for s in out)
    return emit(args.json, out, text) or 0

# A download is the one command that takes minutes, so it says how far along
# it is while it runs. Each download process writes its own small file named
# after the video, which the UI reads and the process removes when it's done:
# no shared file for two downloads to race over, and a process killed halfway
# leaves an entry that readers discard the moment they find its lock free.

# Whether a fetch is still running is asked the same way playback is: by
# taking a lock, never by looking up a pid. A fetch started by the window and
# one started from a terminal may share no more than a filesystem, so the lock
# is the only thing both can be sure of seeing.

def dl_lock(vid):
    return os.path.join(DLDIR, vid + ".lock")

def dl_hold(vid):
    """Claim this song's download for this process, for as long as it runs.
    False if somebody else is already fetching it. The few tries are for a
    reader that has the lock for the instant it takes to test it."""
    for _ in range(10):
        try:
            os.makedirs(DLDIR, exist_ok=True)
            fd = os.open(dl_lock(vid), os.O_RDWR | os.O_CREAT, 0o600)
        except OSError:
            return False
        try:
            fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
            _LOCK_FD.append(fd)         # held until this process ends
            return True
        except OSError:
            os.close(fd)
            time.sleep(0.02)
    return False

def dl_report(vid, stage, percent=None):
    path = os.path.join(DLDIR, vid + ".json")
    try:
        os.makedirs(DLDIR, exist_ok=True)
        with open(path + ".tmp", "w") as f:
            json.dump({"id": vid, "stage": stage,
                       "percent": percent, "updated": time.time()}, f)
        os.replace(path + ".tmp", path)     # atomic: no reader sees half a file
    except OSError:
        pass                                # progress is a nicety, never fatal

def dl_done(vid):
    for path in (os.path.join(DLDIR, vid + ".json"),
                 os.path.join(DLDIR, vid + ".json.tmp"),
                 dl_lock(vid)):
        try:
            os.remove(path)
        except OSError:
            pass

def dl_fail(vid, why=""):
    """Leave a failure where the UI can find it.

    `dl_done` clears the progress file when a fetch is over, which would make
    one that died look exactly like one nobody ever asked for - and a stream
    whose download failed would then sit in the library looking as though it
    were still on its way. It says so in as many words on purpose: the reader
    keeps a failed one rather than tidying it away with the abandoned ones,
    and asking for the song again is what clears it."""
    path = os.path.join(DLDIR, vid + ".json")
    try:
        os.makedirs(DLDIR, exist_ok=True)
        with open(path + ".tmp", "w") as f:
            json.dump({"id": vid, "stage": "failed",
                       "error": str(why)[:200], "updated": time.time()}, f)
        os.replace(path + ".tmp", path)
    except OSError:
        pass

def dl_live(vid):
    """Whether a fetch for this song is already running.

    Asking to stream something that is already coming down is an ordinary
    thing to do - you clicked download, then decided not to wait - and two
    fetches would pull the same file twice and race each other to move it into
    place. A failed fetch is not one of these: it says so, and it holds no
    lock."""
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(DLDIR, vid + ".json")) as f:
            d = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return False
    if d.get("stage") == "failed":
        return False
    return dl_held(vid)

def dl_held(vid):
    """Whether anyone holds this song's download lock. Taking it is the test:
    it succeeds only if nobody has it, and then it is given straight back."""
    try:
        fd = os.open(dl_lock(vid), os.O_RDWR)
    except OSError:
        return False                    # no lock file: nobody is fetching
    try:
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
        fcntl.flock(fd, fcntl.LOCK_UN)
        return False                    # it was free, so its holder is gone
    except OSError:
        return True
    finally:
        os.close(fd)

def dl_opts(dest):
    """The options a download runs under.

    Shared by the fetch and by the resolve that works out what its file will
    be called, because the name is only right if both ask the same question:
    `prepare_filename` answers for the template it is given, so a resolve
    under different options would name a file the download never writes."""
    return {"quiet": True, "no_warnings": True, "noprogress": True,
            # A downloaded song dates from the download, not from whenever the
            # video was uploaded - left to itself yt-dlp stamps the file with
            # what the server said, which would file a song you fetched this
            # morning under a date years old and drop it to the bottom of a
            # library that reads newest first.
            "updatetime": False,
            "format": "bestaudio/best", "writethumbnail": True,
            "outtmpl": {"default": os.path.join(dest, "%(title)s.%(ext)s")},
            "postprocessors": [
                {"key": "FFmpegExtractAudio", "preferredcodec": "mp3",
                 "preferredquality": "0"},
                {"key": "FFmpegMetadata"},
                {"key": "EmbedThumbnail"}]}

def dl_hook(vid):
    """yt-dlp's progress callback, thinned to a few writes a second - it fires
    per chunk, and nothing reads this faster than twice a second anyway.

    Only the fetch has a percentage. The re-encode that follows is ffmpeg
    working through a file with no total to measure against, so it is reported
    as its own stage rather than a number invented to fill the gap."""
    last = [0.0]
    def hook(d):
        status = d.get("status")
        if status == "downloading":
            now = time.time()
            if now - last[0] < 0.25:
                return
            last[0] = now
            total = d.get("total_bytes") or d.get("total_bytes_estimate")
            got = d.get("downloaded_bytes") or 0
            dl_report(vid, "downloading",
                      round(min(100.0, 100.0 * got / total), 1) if total else None)
        elif status == "finished":
            last[0] = 0.0                   # the next file starts its own clock
            dl_report(vid, "converting")
    return hook

def fetch_id(vid):
    """Fetch one video into the library, as (what landed, why not).

    The work `download` does with none of the printing, so that `add` can do
    it too and the two reach the library by exactly one road - which is what
    makes `add PLAYLIST 3` on a YouTube result the same thing as downloading
    it and adding it, rather than a second way of doing nearly that."""
    m = ytdlp()
    if m is None:
        return None, YTDLP_GONE
    if m.version.__version__ < YTDLP_MIN:
        return None, ("yt-dlp %s is too old to download from YouTube - "
                      "update it with `simplmusik update`" % m.version.__version__)
    if not shutil.which("ffmpeg"):
        return None, "ffmpeg isn't installed - it's what writes the mp3"
    dest = folder()             # new songs land in your music folder

    # Played a moment ago? Then the song is already on the disk and this is a
    # rename, with no network in it at all - which is the whole reason playing
    # one fills the cache behind it. And if the cache is still filling, waiting
    # for that beats pulling the same song down a second time alongside it.
    ready = cached_ready(vid)
    if not ready and dl_live(vid):
        waited = 0.0
        while dl_live(vid) and not cached_ready(vid) and waited < CACHE_WAIT:
            time.sleep(0.25)
            waited += 0.25
        ready = cached_ready(vid)
    if ready:
        if ready in index():    # it arrived by some other route in the meantime
            try:
                os.remove(os.path.join(cache_dir(), ready))
                os.remove(os.path.join(cache_dir(), vid + ".json"))
            except OSError:
                pass
            return {"song": ready, "id": vid, "downloaded": False}, None
        promote(vid, ready)
        return {"song": ready, "id": vid, "downloaded": True, "cached": True}, None

    url = "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=" + vid
    # Audio only, re-encoded to mp3 to match the rest of the library, with the
    # tags and the thumbnail written into the file - which is exactly where the
    # UI reads a song's title, artist and cover art from.
    opts = dict(dl_opts(dest), progress_hooks=[dl_hook(vid)])
    os.makedirs(dest, exist_ok=True)
    dl_hold(vid)                # this fetch is ours, for as long as it runs
    dl_report(vid, "starting")
    try:
        with m.YoutubeDL(opts) as y:
            info = y.extract_info(url, download=False)
            # What the file will be called once it is an mp3. Knowing that
            # before fetching anything is what lets a song already in the
            # library cost a second instead of a minute.
            song = os.path.basename(os.path.splitext(y.prepare_filename(info))[0]) + ".mp3"
            if song in index():
                return {"song": song, "id": vid, "downloaded": False}, None
            # Fetch what we just looked up rather than looking it up again.
            # Asking YouTube a second time costs as much as the first ask did
            # - about a second and a half of the eleven this used to take -
            # and it can only ever answer with what we are already holding.
            y.process_ie_result(dict(info), download=True)
    except Exception as e:
        return None, "download failed: %s" % e
    finally:
        dl_done(vid)            # nothing is running now, whichever way we left
    if not os.path.isfile(os.path.join(dest, song)):
        return None, "download finished but %s isn't there" % song
    return {"song": song, "id": vid, "downloaded": True}, None

def added_to(args, name, path):
    """Put a freshly fetched song in the playlist `--to` named, as (playlist,
    what to say about it, whether that worked).

    A playlist that doesn't exist is a failure, and it still says the song is
    in your library: half the job was done and pretending otherwise would be a
    lie about the half that worked. Naming the library is not a failure - it
    is where the song already went."""
    if not args.to:
        return None, None, True
    stem = find(args.to)
    if not stem:
        if is_library(args.to):
            return None, None, True
        return None, "but there is no playlist '%s'" % args.to, False
    return (stem, "and added to " + stem if join(stem, name, path)
            else "which %s already had" % stem, True)

def cmd_download(args):
    """Download a search result into the library, and into a playlist if
    `--to` names one. The library is one flat folder, so the file lands beside
    every other song; `--to` is the second half of what the window's download
    button does from inside a playlist."""
    vid, why = video_ref(args.id)
    if not vid:
        return fail(args.json, why, 1)
    got, why = fetch_id(vid)
    if not got:
        return fail(args.json, why, 1)
    got["playlist"], note, ok = added_to(args, got["song"],
                                         index().get(got["song"]))
    said = ("already in the library: " if not got["downloaded"]
            else "added " if got.get("cached") else "downloaded ") + got["song"]
    emit(args.json, got, said + (" " + note if note else ""))
    return 0 if ok else 1

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ streaming
# Downloading a song takes about eleven seconds, and nearly all of it is ffmpeg
# re-encoding a file that has already landed. None of that has to happen before
# you hear anything: YouTube serves the audio at a URL, and mpv is handed a URL
# exactly the way it is handed a path. So `stream` plays from that URL now and
# fetches the song in the background, and the only wait left is the one ask
# YouTube cannot be talked out of - about a second and a half to look the song
# up. What comes down goes into the cache folder and moves into the library
# once it is whole, so the next time you play it there is no wait at all.

def resolved_get(vid):
    """A lookup from a moment ago, or None if there is none worth trusting."""
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(RESOLVED, vid + ".json")) as f:
            got = json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    if not got.get("url") or time.time() - (got.get("at") or 0) > RESOLVE_TTL:
        return None
    return got

def resolved_put(vid, url, song):
    """Keep a lookup for a little while.

    In this machine's own state folder, and not in your music folder with the
    songs and the playlists. A stream URL is tied to the address that asked
    for it and goes stale within hours, so it is the one thing here that must
    not be carried to another machine along with the music."""
    if not url:
        return
    try:
        os.makedirs(RESOLVED, exist_ok=True)
        path = os.path.join(RESOLVED, vid + ".json")
        with open(path + ".tmp", "w") as f:
            json.dump({"id": vid, "url": url, "song": song, "at": time.time()}, f)
        os.replace(path + ".tmp", path)     # atomic: no reader sees half a file
        sweep_resolved()
    except OSError:
        pass

def sweep_resolved():
    """Drop lookups nothing could use any more. They are small and few, so
    this rides along on writing one rather than needing a moment of its own."""
    now = time.time()
    try:
        for n in os.listdir(RESOLVED):
            f = os.path.join(RESOLVED, n)
            try:
                if now - os.path.getmtime(f) > RESOLVE_TTL:
                    os.remove(f)
            except OSError:
                pass
    except OSError:
        pass

def resolve(vid):
    """Look a song up: where to stream it from, and what its file will be
    called when it lands.

    Both answers come out of the same extraction on purpose. The name is
    `prepare_filename` reading the template it was given, so it is only the
    name the download will really write if the two ask under the same
    options - which is what `dl_opts` is for.

    A lookup made a moment ago is used again rather than made again, and that
    is the whole of what makes a streamed song start at once rather than a
    second and a half later: the window has the server look the first result
    up while you are still reading the list, so by the time you press play
    there is nothing left to ask anybody."""
    got = resolved_get(vid)
    if got:
        return None, got["url"], got["song"]
    m = ytdlp()
    opts = dict(dl_opts(folder()))
    opts["skip_download"] = True
    with m.YoutubeDL(opts) as y:
        info = y.extract_info("https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=" + vid,
                              download=False)
        song = os.path.basename(os.path.splitext(y.prepare_filename(info))[0]) + ".mp3"
    resolved_put(vid, info.get("url"), song)
    return info, info.get("url"), song

def fetch(vid, song):
    """The detached process that fills the cache behind a song being played.

    Nobody asked for this download, so it says nothing while it runs: playing
    a song is playing a song, and a progress ring on a row you only wanted to
    hear would be reporting work you never asked to wait for. It writes into
    the cache and stops there. Moving it into the library is `download`'s job,
    and by then there is nothing left to fetch - which is the point of it.

    The stage it reports is one the UI passes over. It is written all the same,
    so that `download` can tell a song already on its way from one nobody has
    started, and wait for it instead of pulling the same bytes twice."""
    m = ytdlp()
    if m is None:
        return 1
    cache = cache_dir()
    if os.path.isfile(os.path.join(folder(), song)):
        dl_done(vid)            # already a library song: nothing worth caching
        return 0
    got = os.path.join(cache, song)
    dl_hold(vid)                # this fetch is ours, for as long as it runs
    dl_report(vid, "caching")
    try:
        os.makedirs(cache, exist_ok=True)
        with m.YoutubeDL(dl_opts(cache)) as y:
            info = y.extract_info("https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=" + vid,
                                  download=False)
            y.process_ie_result(dict(info), download=True)
        if not os.path.isfile(got):
            raise OSError("the fetch finished but %s isn't there" % song)
        # The name is written beside the file because working it out again
        # means asking YouTube, and a cache you have to make a network call to
        # use is not the thing that makes `download` quick.
        with open(os.path.join(cache, vid + ".json"), "w") as f:
            json.dump({"song": song, "cached": time.time()}, f)
    except Exception:
        # Silent in, silent out. Nobody is waiting on this, and a song that
        # failed to cache simply costs a download later, the way it always did.
        for junk in (got, os.path.join(cache, vid + ".json")):
            try:
                os.remove(junk)
            except OSError:
                pass
        dl_done(vid)
        return 1
    dl_done(vid)
    return 0

def keep(vid, song):
    """The detached process behind `stream --to`: wait for whatever is
    fetching this song, then move it into the library.

    Streaming fills the cache and stops there - moving a song into the library
    is `download`'s job, and the cache is swept after a day. That is the right
    bargain for a song you only wanted to hear, and the wrong one for a song
    you asked to have in a playlist: the row would sit there saying 'not
    found' until the file it names was thrown away. So asking for the playlist
    is what asks for the promote, and this is the part of it that has to
    outlive the command.

    It waits rather than fetching, because something is already fetching -
    the process `stream` started a moment ago, or one that was running before
    either of us. Whoever it is writes the same cache, so there is nothing to
    do but let them finish.

    A lock that isn't there yet is what the grace is for. `stream` starts that
    fetch and this in the same breath, and they race: with no grace, an empty
    cache and an unclaimed song would read as 'nobody is fetching this' in the
    moment before the fetch has said otherwise."""
    waited = 0.0
    while waited < CACHE_WAIT:
        if song in index():
            return 0                # it got there by some other road
        ready = cached_ready(vid)
        if ready:
            promote(vid, ready)
            return 0
        if waited > CACHE_GRACE and not dl_live(vid):
            return 1                # nothing is coming, and nothing is holding it
        time.sleep(0.5)
        waited += 0.5
    return 1

def cached_ready(vid):
    """The song this video is cached as, if a whole copy is sitting there
    waiting to be claimed. None while it is still coming down: the name is
    only written once the file is complete, so a half-fetched song can never
    be mistaken for one ready to move."""
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(cache_dir(), vid + ".json")) as f:
            song = json.load(f).get("song")
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    return song if song and os.path.isfile(os.path.join(cache_dir(), song)) else None

def promote(vid, song):
    """Move a cached song into the library.

    A rename on one filesystem - the cache is inside your music folder for
    exactly this - so the song is in the cache or in the library and never
    halfway between either. The note naming it goes too: the file it pointed
    at has left, and a note pointing at nothing would have `download` offer a
    song it can no longer produce."""
    dest = folder()
    os.makedirs(dest, exist_ok=True)
    final = os.path.join(dest, song)
    os.replace(os.path.join(cache_dir(), song), final)
    try:
        os.remove(os.path.join(cache_dir(), vid + ".json"))
    except OSError:
        pass
    return final

def cmd_stream(args):
    """Play a search result now, and fetch it while it plays.

    A song already in the library is played from the disk instead - the stream
    would be slower and worse, and there is nothing left to download."""
    m = ytdlp()
    if m is None:
        return fail(args.json, YTDLP_GONE, 1)
    if m.version.__version__ < YTDLP_MIN:
        return fail(args.json, "yt-dlp %s is too old to stream from YouTube - "
                    "update it with `simplmusik update`"
                    % m.version.__version__, 1)
    if not shutil.which("mpv"):
        return fail(args.json, "mpv not found - install mpv", 1)
    vid, why = video_ref(args.id)
    if not vid:
        return fail(args.json, why, 1)
    sweep_cache()
    running = dl_live(vid)      # already coming down: stream it, don't re-fetch
    if not running:
        dl_done(vid)            # asking again clears what last time left behind
    try:
        info, url, song = resolve(vid)
    except Exception as e:      # yt-dlp raises a wide family of these
        return fail(args.json, "couldn't start that song: %s" % e, 1)
    if not url:
        return fail(args.json, "no audio stream for that song", 2)

    # Three places the song might already be, in the order worth trying: the
    # library, then the cache from having played it before, then YouTube. Only
    # the last of them is a stream, and only the last is worth caching.
    source = index().get(song)
    if not source and cached_ready(vid) == song:
        source = os.path.join(cache_dir(), song)
    live = not source               # nothing local: play it off the network
    if live:
        source = url
    if live and not running:
        if not shutil.which("ffmpeg"):
            return fail(args.json, "ffmpeg isn't installed - it's what writes the mp3", 1)
        subprocess.Popen(
            [sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__), "_fetch", vid, song],
            start_new_session=True, stdin=NULL, stdout=NULL, stderr=NULL)

    # One song, and the queue is the same shape `play` builds - the player
    # loop can't tell this job from any other, and doesn't need to.
    job = {"playlist": None, "songs": [song], "paths": {song: source},
           "order": [song], "index": 0, "song": song,
           "paused": False, "shuffle": False, "step": 1, "seq": 0,
           "vid": vid}        # what to look for in the cache, if it lands
    if not start_playing(job):
        return fail(args.json, "couldn't start the player", 1)
    # `--to` puts it in the playlist now rather than when the bytes land. The
    # name is already settled - `resolve` worked out what the file will be
    # called before a byte of it arrived - and a playlist naming a song that
    # isn't there yet is a row that says so and then stops saying it, which is
    # the same not-found row a song you moved on the disk gets.
    to, note, ok = added_to(args, song, index().get(song))
    if args.to and ok and song not in index():
        # Asked to be kept, and not kept yet. The playlist has the song in it
        # already - the name was settled before a byte arrived - and this is
        # what makes that row come true rather than go stale.
        subprocess.Popen(
            [sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__), "_keep", vid, song],
            start_new_session=True, stdin=NULL, stdout=NULL, stderr=NULL)
    emit(args.json, {"song": song, "id": vid, "streaming": live, "playlist": to},
         ("streaming " if live else "playing ") + song + (" " + note if note else ""))
    return 0 if ok else 1

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ updating
# The one part of this that goes stale on its own. Everything else here is
# finished once it works; yt-dlp is in a race with YouTube, and the copy that
# played a song last month is the one that finds nothing but thumbnails today.

def cmd_update(args):
    """Fetch the newest yt-dlp.

    It lands in your own folder rather than beside the app, which is what lets
    this work the same whether simplmusik was unpacked into a folder you own
    or installed as a package owned by root. `ytdlp` prefers whichever copy is
    newest, so the fetched one simply wins from here on."""
    import urllib.request                # only this command ever needs it
    was = zipapp_version(YTDLP_USER) or zipapp_version(YTDLP_BUNDLED)
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(YTDLP_URL, timeout=180) as r:
            blob = r.read()
    except Exception as e:               # urllib raises a wide family of these
        return fail(args.json, "couldn't fetch yt-dlp: %s" % e, 1)

    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(YTDLP_USER), exist_ok=True)
    tmp = YTDLP_USER + ".tmp"
    try:
        with open(tmp, "wb") as f:
            f.write(blob)
        got = zipapp_version(tmp)
        if not got:
            # A captive portal or a proxy answers every request with something.
            # Checking that what came back is a yt-dlp is what keeps that
            # something from being written over a working one.
            os.remove(tmp)
            return fail(args.json, "what came back isn't a yt-dlp - check the network", 1)
        os.chmod(tmp, 0o755)
        os.replace(tmp, YTDLP_USER)      # atomic: no half file is ever run
    except OSError as e:
        return fail(args.json, "couldn't write %s: %s" % (YTDLP_USER, e), 1)

    _ytdlp[:] = []                       # decided already, and now out of date
    return emit(args.json, {"version": got, "was": was, "path": YTDLP_USER},
                "yt-dlp is now %s%s" % (got, "" if was in (None, got)
                                        else " (was %s)" % was)) or 0

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- main
# name: (help, positional args, optional flag). "?" = optional, "+" = one or more.

# Every command that takes a song takes it three ways, and it is worth saying
# so where somebody is already asking - `help add` - rather than only in the
# notes. `download` and `stream` take a video instead, and a number there means
# a result of the last YouTube search rather than a song you already have.

BY_SONG = ("a song can be named three ways: the number beside it in the last "
           "list you were shown\n(search, songs, list), its whole filename, or "
           "enough of the filename to tell\nit from every other song.")

BY_TOPIC = ("topics:\n"
            "  summary     everything at a glance (the default)\n"
            "  songs       every song, ranked - --by time, plays, skips or recent\n"
            "  playlists   the same for the lists you played them out of\n"
            "  skips       how far into a song you leave it, and which ones\n"
            "  time        the hours you listen in, and your last few evenings\n"
            "  log         the spans themselves, newest first")

BY_VIDEO = ("a video can be named three ways: the number beside it in the last "
            "`search --yt`,\nits YouTube id, or a link to it.")

COMMANDS = [
    ("status",  "library + playback summary",                     "",                None),
    ("stats",   "what you have actually listened to",             "topic?",          "top since by"),
    ("folder",  "show your music folder, or choose another",      "action? path?",   None),
    ("songs",   "list every song in the library, newest first",   "",                None),
    ("list",    "list playlists, or the songs in one (\"%s\" is the library)"
                % LIBRARY_NAME,                                  "playlist?",       None),
    ("create",  "make a new, empty playlist",                     "name",            "cover"),
    ("rename",  "give a playlist a different name",               "playlist name",   None),
    ("cover",   "show, set or clear a playlist's cover art",      "playlist image?", "clear"),
    ("add",     "add songs to a playlist, by number, name or path", "playlist files+", None),
    ("search",  "search your library, or YouTube with --yt",      "query+",          "count yt"),
    ("download","download a search result into the library",      "id",              "to"),
    ("stream",  "play a search result now, and fetch it as it plays", "id",          "to"),
    ("remove",  "drop a song from a playlist, or delete it",      "playlist song?",  None),
    ("delete",  "delete a playlist (its songs stay)",             "playlist",        None),
    ("play",    "play a playlist, one song, or the library",      "playlist? song?", "shuffle library"),
    ("shuffle", "play in a random order, until you turn it off",   "action?",         None),
    ("levelling","play every song at one loudness",               "action?",         None),
    ("target",  "the loudness levelling aims for, in dB",          "db?",             None),
    ("volume",  "how loud, 0-100 (100 is as levelled)",            "level?",          None),
    ("pause",   "pause playback",                                 "",                None),
    ("resume",  "resume playback",                                "",                None),
    ("skip",    "skip to the next song",                          "",                None),
    ("back",    "go back to the previous song",                   "",                None),
    ("seek",    "jump within the song (1:23, 83, +30, -10)",      "time",            None),
    ("stop",    "stop playback",                                  "",                None),
    ("update",  "fetch the newest yt-dlp, which YouTube keeps needing", "",       None),
    ("help",    "show this help, or help for one command",        "topic?",          None),
]

CMDS = {"status": cmd_status, "stats": cmd_stats, "songs": cmd_songs, "list": cmd_list,
        "folder": cmd_folder, "levelling": cmd_levelling, "volume": cmd_volume,
        "target": cmd_target,
        "create": cmd_create, "add": cmd_add, "remove": cmd_remove,
        "rename": cmd_rename, "cover": cmd_cover,
        "search": cmd_search, "download": cmd_download, "stream": cmd_stream,
        "delete": cmd_delete, "play": cmd_play, "shuffle": cmd_shuffle,
        "seek": cmd_seek, "stop": cmd_stop, "pause": cmd_pause,
        "resume": lambda args: cmd_pause(args, want=False),
        "skip": lambda args: cmd_move(args, "skip"),
        "back": lambda args: cmd_move(args, "back"),
        "update": cmd_update}

def main(argv=None):
    argv = sys.argv[1:] if argv is None else argv
    if argv[:1] == ["_player"]:         # internal: the detached player process
        job = load_json(argv[1], None)  # cmd_play left this for us, and for no
        try:                            # one else, so it goes as soon as it is
            os.remove(argv[1])          # read - nothing stale is left behind
        except OSError:
            pass
        if not job:                 # no queue to run: nothing to play
            return 1
        return player(job) or 0
    if argv[:1] == ["_fetch"]:          # internal: the detached download that
        return fetch(argv[1], argv[2])  # runs behind a song being streamed
    if argv[:1] == ["_keep"]:           # internal: the wait-and-promote behind
        return keep(argv[1], argv[2])   # `stream --to`, which wants it kept
    migrate()                           # playlists moved folder once, long ago
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="simplmusik", description=__doc__,
                                 formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    ap.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="machine-readable output")
    sub = ap.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)
    for name, help_, positional, flag in COMMANDS:
        p = sub.add_parser(name, help=help_,
                           epilog={"add": BY_SONG, "play": BY_SONG,
                                   "remove": BY_SONG, "download": BY_VIDEO,
                                   "stream": BY_VIDEO, "stats": BY_TOPIC}.get(name),
                           formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
        for spec in positional.split():
            p.add_argument(spec.rstrip("?+"),
                           nargs={"?": "?", "+": "+"}.get(spec[-1]))
        for f in (flag or "").split():
            if f == "cover":
                p.add_argument("--cover", help="an image to use as its cover art")
            elif f == "clear":
                p.add_argument("--clear", action="store_true",
                               help="take the cover art off again")
            elif f == "shuffle":
                p.add_argument("--shuffle", action="store_true",
                               help="turn shuffle on, and play shuffled")
            elif f == "library":
                p.add_argument("--library", action="store_true",
                               help="play the whole library, whatever is on now")
            elif f == "count":
                # No default: a library search shows everything it found,
                # because it cost nothing to find, and only YouTube has a
                # handful to fall back on.
                p.add_argument("--count", type=int,
                               help="how many results to bring back "
                                    "(up to %d from YouTube)" % SEARCH_MAX)
            elif f == "yt":
                # `-yt` as well as `--yt`: it is what everybody types, and
                # argparse will take a long option spelled with one dash.
                p.add_argument("--yt", "-yt", action="store_true",
                               help="search YouTube instead of your library")
            elif f == "top":
                p.add_argument("--count", type=int, metavar="N",
                               help="how many rows to show")
            elif f == "since":
                p.add_argument("--since", metavar="WINDOW",
                               help="only count this far back: 7d, 24h, 2w, all")
            elif f == "by":
                p.add_argument("--by", metavar="ORDER",
                               help="order the tables: time (default), plays, "
                                    "skips, recent")
            elif f == "to":
                p.add_argument("--to", metavar="PLAYLIST",
                               help="put it in this playlist as well as the library")

    args = ap.parse_args(argv)
    if args.cmd == "help":
        if not args.topic:
            return ap.print_help() or 0
        if args.topic not in sub.choices:
            print("no such command: " + args.topic, file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        return sub.choices[args.topic].print_help() or 0
    return CMDS[args.cmd](args)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
